1
00:03:16,446 --> 00:03:18,072
You see how it's done?

2
00:03:18,240 --> 00:03:20,783
Yeah, I think I got the hang of it.

3
00:03:20,951 --> 00:03:25,621
You better. The boss will chew my head off
if these cores get messed up.

4
00:03:25,789 --> 00:03:27,456
Don't worry.

5
00:03:41,471 --> 00:03:43,639
We're at 26 feet.

6
00:03:43,807 --> 00:03:45,558
You let Jason operate the drill?

7
00:03:45,726 --> 00:03:47,810
Yeah, he can handle it.

8
00:04:38,528 --> 00:04:40,654
I didn't do anything.

9
00:04:46,995 --> 00:04:50,206
Give me your hand!
Let go of the drill!

10
00:04:57,881 --> 00:05:00,633
Forget it, Jack! It's too late!

11
00:05:12,896 --> 00:05:14,146
You're not gonna make it!

12
00:05:25,283 --> 00:05:26,534
Jack!

13
00:05:37,087 --> 00:05:39,839
Jack! Give me your hand!

14
00:05:44,052 --> 00:05:46,095
I've got you!

15
00:05:48,765 --> 00:05:50,224
What were you thinking?

16
00:05:52,143 --> 00:05:56,564
- What's happening?
- The whole damn shelf is breaking off!

17
00:05:56,815 --> 00:05:58,941
That's what's happening!

18
00:06:17,002 --> 00:06:20,796
We found evidence of a cataclysmic climate
shift, which occurred 10,000 years ago.

19
00:06:24,259 --> 00:06:28,137
The concentration of these natural
greenhouse gases in the ice cores...

20
00:06:28,305 --> 00:06:31,640
...indicates that runaway warming
pushed Earth into an ice age...

21
00:06:31,808 --> 00:06:34,101
...which lasted two centuries.

22
00:06:35,437 --> 00:06:37,313
I'm confused.

23
00:06:37,480 --> 00:06:41,483
I thought you were talking about
global warming, not an ice age.

24
00:06:41,651 --> 00:06:48,032
Yes, it is a paradox, but global warming can
trigger a cooling trend. Let me explain.

25
00:06:48,491 --> 00:06:53,245
The Northern Hemisphere owes its climate
to the North Atlantic Current.

26
00:06:53,413 --> 00:06:57,625
Heat from the sun arrives at the equator
and is carried north by the ocean.

27
00:06:57,876 --> 00:07:03,380
But global warming is melting the polar
ice caps and disrupting this flow.

28
00:07:03,548 --> 00:07:07,134
Eventually it will shut down.
And when that occurs...

29
00:07:07,302 --> 00:07:09,178
...there goes our warm climate.

30
00:07:09,346 --> 00:07:15,142
Excuse me. When do you think this
could happen, professor? When?

31
00:07:15,310 --> 00:07:19,271
I don't know. Maybe in 100 years,
maybe in 1000. But what I do know is...

32
00:07:19,439 --> 00:07:24,777
...that if we do not act soon, our children
and grandchildren will have to pay the price.

33
00:07:26,404 --> 00:07:29,490
And who's going to pay the price
of the Kyoto Accord?

34
00:07:29,658 --> 00:07:33,535
It would cost the world's economy
hundreds of billions of dollars.

35
00:07:34,079 --> 00:07:39,166
With all due respect, Mr. Vice President, the
cost of doing nothing could be even higher.

36
00:07:39,876 --> 00:07:41,669
Our climate is fragile.

37
00:07:41,836 --> 00:07:45,047
At the rate we're burning fossil fuels
and polluting the environment...

38
00:07:45,215 --> 00:07:47,424
...the ice caps will soon disappear.

39
00:07:47,592 --> 00:07:50,928
Professor Hall...

40
00:07:51,096 --> 00:07:54,181
...our economy is every bit as fragile
as the environment.

41
00:07:54,349 --> 00:07:58,394
Perhaps you should keep that in mind
before making sensationalist claims.

42
00:07:58,770 --> 00:08:02,064
Well, the last chunk of ice
that broke off...

43
00:08:02,232 --> 00:08:04,400
...was about the size of Rhode Island.

44
00:08:04,567 --> 00:08:07,403
Some people might call that
pretty sensational.

45
00:08:09,698 --> 00:08:12,992
Stop global warming!
Stop global warming!

46
00:08:13,702 --> 00:08:16,328
I'm at the Global Warming Conference
in New Delhi...

47
00:08:16,496 --> 00:08:19,039
...where, if you can believe it,
it's snowing.

48
00:08:19,207 --> 00:08:22,793
The coldest weather on record
has thrown the city into chaos...

49
00:08:22,961 --> 00:08:25,754
...with numbers of homeless people
freezing to death.

50
00:08:25,922 --> 00:08:27,423
Taxi!

51
00:08:27,590 --> 00:08:31,260
I enjoyed your testimony, professor.
It was very spirited.

52
00:08:31,428 --> 00:08:34,930
Oh, thank you. That's what we're
here for, right? Put on a good show?

53
00:08:35,098 --> 00:08:40,978
Quite. I was wondering if I could talk to you
about your theory on abrupt climate shift.

54
00:08:41,146 --> 00:08:43,439
The name's Rapson. Terry Rapson.

55
00:08:43,982 --> 00:08:46,650
- Professor Rapson? Of the Hedland Center?
- That's me.

56
00:08:46,818 --> 00:08:49,528
I've read your work on ocean currents.

57
00:08:49,696 --> 00:08:54,366
- What do you say to a spot of tea?
- Absolutely. If we can hail a cab.

58
00:08:57,328 --> 00:08:59,038
Over here.

59
00:09:17,682 --> 00:09:20,768
Welcome back to Glasgow, Scotland,
where Manchester United...

60
00:09:20,935 --> 00:09:23,562
...leads 3-1 over hometown Celtic.

61
00:09:25,940 --> 00:09:29,651
We return 63 minutes into
the second half as Manchester United...

62
00:09:29,819 --> 00:09:33,697
...looks to put the game out of reach.
Let's get back to our commentator...

63
00:09:33,865 --> 00:09:35,699
...Donald MacFarland.

64
00:09:36,951 --> 00:09:39,286
What? Yeah.

65
00:09:39,454 --> 00:09:42,247
- I just closed my eyes for a sec, man.
- Yeah.

66
00:09:42,415 --> 00:09:44,833
The baby kept us awake all night.

67
00:09:45,001 --> 00:09:47,836
- And still.
- Yeah!

68
00:09:58,765 --> 00:10:04,686
Dennis? NOMAD buoy 4311 is showing
a temperature drop of 13 degrees.

69
00:10:05,021 --> 00:10:08,315
- Yeah? Where is 4311?
- Well, it's...

70
00:10:12,529 --> 00:10:15,322
- Georges Bank.
- It's rough seas out there.

71
00:10:15,490 --> 00:10:17,199
Must have knocked it about.

72
00:10:29,212 --> 00:10:31,880
Kick that bloody ball. Come on!

73
00:10:32,048 --> 00:10:34,508
Come on, kick it now. Kick it!

74
00:10:34,884 --> 00:10:36,552
- Kick it!
- Are the lads winning?

75
00:10:37,762 --> 00:10:40,514
Hello, professor. How was India?

76
00:10:40,682 --> 00:10:43,225
Oh, you know what these
scientific gatherings are.

77
00:10:43,393 --> 00:10:46,395
All dancing girls, wine and parties.

78
00:12:28,831 --> 00:12:32,584
The fury of Hurricane Noelani
stunned weather experts yesterday...

79
00:12:32,752 --> 00:12:37,798
...slamming into the island chain with a
cataclysmic force never before witnessed.

80
00:12:37,966 --> 00:12:42,344
Meteorologists already believe this to be
the strongest hurricane ever recorded...

81
00:12:43,429 --> 00:12:44,888
Are you gonna get that?

82
00:12:45,056 --> 00:12:47,224
It will surely leave a wake of death...

83
00:12:47,392 --> 00:12:51,853
- Hello?
- I just saw that Sam got an F in calculus.

84
00:12:52,021 --> 00:12:55,357
I'm aware, Jack.
I get a copy of his report card too.

85
00:12:55,525 --> 00:12:58,568
Sam is a straight-A student.
He doesn't fail classes.

86
00:12:58,736 --> 00:13:00,862
I don't have time to talk about this now.

87
00:13:01,030 --> 00:13:03,865
Well, maybe you ought to make time.

88
00:13:04,033 --> 00:13:08,036
Excuse me, I'm not the one who's away
for months and months at a time.

89
00:13:08,204 --> 00:13:09,913
I just don't understand.

90
00:13:10,123 --> 00:13:13,834
I'll let him explain it. Can you take him
to the airport in the morning?

91
00:13:14,002 --> 00:13:17,754
- Sam's getting on a plane?
- He joined the Scholastic Decathlon Team.

92
00:13:17,922 --> 00:13:22,551
- They're competing in New York.
- Sam joined a team?

93
00:13:23,052 --> 00:13:25,387
- Yeah, I think there's a girl involved.
- Oh.

94
00:13:25,555 --> 00:13:27,306
Look, can you pick him up at 8:30?

95
00:13:27,640 --> 00:13:29,808
I gotta go because I'm on call tonight.

96
00:13:29,976 --> 00:13:32,728
Don't be late. I don't want
him taking a taxi again.

97
00:13:32,895 --> 00:13:36,982
All right. Okay. I'll be there.
Okay? I'll be there.

98
00:13:40,570 --> 00:13:44,489
This morning's weather staff meeting
has been moved to level four, room B.

99
00:13:47,577 --> 00:13:49,202
Jack?

100
00:13:54,125 --> 00:13:56,793
I know you're good at rubbing people
the wrong way...

101
00:13:56,961 --> 00:14:00,172
...but why would you aggravate
the vice president?

102
00:14:00,340 --> 00:14:03,300
Because my 17-year-old kid
knows more science than he does.

103
00:14:03,468 --> 00:14:06,136
Your 17-year-old kid
does not control our budget.

104
00:14:06,304 --> 00:14:08,930
- Who cares if he hates you.
- My son doesn't hate me.

105
00:14:09,098 --> 00:14:11,016
If Raymond Becker pulls our budget-

106
00:14:11,184 --> 00:14:15,520
- Oh, shit!
- Wait- Will you-? Jack.

107
00:14:21,277 --> 00:14:23,445
Oh, my God.

108
00:14:29,452 --> 00:14:32,120
- I'm sorry I'm late.
- Dad, the cab's already here.

109
00:14:32,288 --> 00:14:34,247
That's okay. I'll take care of it.

110
00:14:34,415 --> 00:14:35,749
- What are-?
- Here you go.

111
00:14:38,211 --> 00:14:41,463
I'm not angry. I'm disappointed.

112
00:14:41,631 --> 00:14:44,841
- Do you want to hear my side of it?
- How can there be two sides?

113
00:14:45,009 --> 00:14:48,762
I got every question right on the final.
Mr. Spengler failed me...

114
00:14:48,930 --> 00:14:51,473
- ... because I didn't write the solutions.
- Why not?

115
00:14:51,974 --> 00:14:54,184
I do them in my head.

116
00:14:54,352 --> 00:14:57,145
- Did you tell him that?
- I did. He didn't believe me.

117
00:14:57,313 --> 00:15:00,148
He said if he can't do them in his head,
I'm cheating.

118
00:15:00,566 --> 00:15:03,652
Ridiculous. How can he fail you
for being smarter than he is?

119
00:15:04,070 --> 00:15:06,363
That's what I said.

120
00:15:06,823 --> 00:15:08,281
You did?

121
00:15:10,034 --> 00:15:12,536
- How'd he take it?
- He flunked me, remember?

122
00:15:13,871 --> 00:15:15,789
Oh, yeah.

123
00:15:18,459 --> 00:15:22,963
Sam, I'm sorry.
I jumped to conclusions.

124
00:15:23,673 --> 00:15:27,134
I'm gonna call this guy
and have a word with him.

125
00:15:27,301 --> 00:15:28,635
We'll straighten this out.

126
00:15:28,803 --> 00:15:30,512
Hey, you can't park there.

127
00:15:30,680 --> 00:15:32,889
- Don't worry about it.
- Sam?

128
00:15:33,391 --> 00:15:35,100
Sam.

129
00:15:49,198 --> 00:15:51,575
Parker, this is Houston.

130
00:15:51,742 --> 00:15:54,494
We're seeing some bad weather
over Canaveral.

131
00:15:54,662 --> 00:15:57,289
It doesn't look like you're coming back
this week.

132
00:15:57,457 --> 00:15:59,541
Your wife's gonna give me an earful.

133
00:15:59,709 --> 00:16:01,334
Roger that.

134
00:16:01,502 --> 00:16:05,672
Hey, come take a look at this storm system.
It's enormous.

135
00:16:39,040 --> 00:16:40,874
You all right?

136
00:16:43,461 --> 00:16:45,420
He's afraid of flying.

137
00:16:45,588 --> 00:16:47,297
I'm fine.

138
00:16:55,348 --> 00:16:59,142
Statistically, the chance of a plane
going down because of turbulence...

139
00:16:59,310 --> 00:17:03,855
...is less than, what, one in a billion?
Or is it a million?

140
00:17:04,815 --> 00:17:07,234
- I can't remember if it's a-
- Shut up, Brian.

141
00:17:07,401 --> 00:17:08,818
Listen, Sam...

142
00:17:08,986 --> 00:17:11,905
...don't pay attention to him, okay?
Everything's fine.

143
00:17:12,073 --> 00:17:14,866
They're still serving drinks.

144
00:17:28,339 --> 00:17:32,467
Folks, it appears we're gonna have a bit
of a bumpy ride for the next few minutes.

145
00:17:32,635 --> 00:17:35,679
Please fasten your seat belts
and put your tray tables...

146
00:17:35,846 --> 00:17:40,350
...and seat backs in their upright positions
until we get through this. Thank you.

147
00:17:41,143 --> 00:17:42,477
Whoa!

148
00:17:42,645 --> 00:17:44,646
Grab it. Watch out!

149
00:18:15,595 --> 00:18:16,970
Sam?

150
00:18:18,347 --> 00:18:19,973
Sam.

151
00:18:23,311 --> 00:18:24,936
Can I have my hand back?

152
00:18:41,537 --> 00:18:43,747
I can't believe I'll be alone
with my mom.

153
00:18:43,914 --> 00:18:47,125
Be patient with her.
She's been looking forward to this holiday.

154
00:18:47,293 --> 00:18:48,752
I know.

155
00:18:49,045 --> 00:18:51,004
I love you.

156
00:18:51,881 --> 00:18:53,173
I love you too.

157
00:18:55,217 --> 00:18:56,718
Hey.

158
00:18:57,011 --> 00:18:59,387
Hey, hello. Bye-bye.

159
00:18:59,555 --> 00:19:03,808
The cause of this extreme weather remains
a mystery, although some meteorologists...

160
00:19:03,976 --> 00:19:06,895
...believe sunspots are to blame.
Hundreds are missing...

161
00:19:07,063 --> 00:19:12,233
This is very odd. There's a buoy registering
a 13-degree drop in ocean temperature.

162
00:19:12,401 --> 00:19:16,529
Oh, yeah, that's right.
That buoy malfunctioned the other day.

163
00:19:16,697 --> 00:19:19,574
I'll see if there are ships near
Georges Bank to get it.

164
00:19:19,742 --> 00:19:22,911
This buoy isn't in Georges Bank.
It's just off Greenland.

165
00:19:23,079 --> 00:19:24,412
What?

166
00:19:27,208 --> 00:19:30,877
- What are the odds of two buoys failing?
- Remote.

167
00:19:36,842 --> 00:19:38,677
Make that three.

168
00:19:49,313 --> 00:19:54,401
Just another typical day in New York City.
Traffic jam, 10 blocks long.

169
00:19:54,568 --> 00:19:59,823
Look here, Buddha. These people,
and their cars, and their exhaust...

170
00:19:59,990 --> 00:20:02,033
...and they're polluting the atmosphere.

171
00:20:02,201 --> 00:20:05,829
Excuse me, sir. We're really late.

172
00:20:06,122 --> 00:20:08,206
We're almost there.

173
00:20:08,374 --> 00:20:12,544
- We're only two blocks away.
- Let's walk.

174
00:20:39,155 --> 00:20:40,822
What's gotten into them?

175
00:20:40,990 --> 00:20:45,493
I have no idea.
They're all worked up today.

176
00:20:46,704 --> 00:20:51,875
In 1532, Spanish conquistador
Francisco Pizarro...

177
00:20:52,042 --> 00:20:57,672
...defeated this Incan emperor at the
Peruvian highland town of Cajamarca.

178
00:20:58,007 --> 00:21:00,717
What is his name? Time.

179
00:21:01,802 --> 00:21:03,303
Montezuma.

180
00:21:03,471 --> 00:21:06,181
No, no, Montezuma was in Mexico,
not Peru.

181
00:21:06,348 --> 00:21:09,309
It's, like, Anta-something.

182
00:21:11,228 --> 00:21:13,146
- Atahualpa?
- That's it!

183
00:21:13,314 --> 00:21:15,148
Time's up. Correct answers, please.

184
00:21:18,235 --> 00:21:23,907
That's five points for Woodmont
and five points for Pinehurst Academy.

185
00:21:24,992 --> 00:21:26,451
Next question.

186
00:21:28,204 --> 00:21:34,250
In what year did Louis Quatorze
ascend to the throne of France?

187
00:21:38,547 --> 00:21:44,385
This place is so retro, it might actually
be cool if it were on purpose.

188
00:21:45,888 --> 00:21:48,223
Yeah, look at all these nerds.

189
00:21:48,390 --> 00:21:52,477
Hey.

190
00:21:54,104 --> 00:21:56,189
You look beautiful.

191
00:21:56,899 --> 00:21:59,067
Thanks. This place is incredible.

192
00:21:59,235 --> 00:22:01,236
Do you believe this is their cafeteria?

193
00:22:01,403 --> 00:22:03,154
You played a great first round.

194
00:22:03,572 --> 00:22:05,281
So did you.

195
00:22:05,449 --> 00:22:08,243
These are my teammates,
Sam and Brian.

196
00:22:08,410 --> 00:22:10,453
- I'm Laura.
- Oh, I'm J. D.

197
00:22:10,621 --> 00:22:13,373
- Your school's amazing.
- Would you like a tour?

198
00:22:17,044 --> 00:22:19,671
Sure. That'd be great.

199
00:22:19,839 --> 00:22:21,798
Could you hold this for a sec?

200
00:22:22,591 --> 00:22:24,759
Yeah, sure.

201
00:22:24,969 --> 00:22:26,386
Thanks.

202
00:22:32,476 --> 00:22:34,853
Man, you got some serious competition.

203
00:22:35,437 --> 00:22:37,355
Please.

204
00:22:37,523 --> 00:22:41,234
- And I'll bet he's really rich too.
- Shut up.

205
00:22:53,497 --> 00:22:56,833
- Who is it?
- Terry Rapson here.

206
00:22:57,001 --> 00:22:58,459
Sorry to call you so early.

207
00:22:58,627 --> 00:23:00,795
No, professor, it's all right.
What is it?

208
00:23:01,672 --> 00:23:06,634
Well, we've found something extraordinary.
Extraordinary and disturbing, that is.

209
00:23:06,802 --> 00:23:10,263
You recall what you said in New Delhi
about how polar melting...

210
00:23:10,431 --> 00:23:14,225
- ... might disrupt the North Atlantic Current?
- Yes.

211
00:23:14,685 --> 00:23:16,144
Well...

212
00:23:17,146 --> 00:23:18,730
...I think it's happening.

213
00:23:22,192 --> 00:23:23,484
What do you mean?

214
00:23:23,652 --> 00:23:27,655
One of our NOMAD buoys registered
a 13-degree drop...

215
00:23:27,823 --> 00:23:30,783
...in surface temperature.
I've sent you an e-mail.

216
00:23:30,951 --> 00:23:32,785
Hold on.

217
00:23:34,163 --> 00:23:36,539
At first we thought it was a malfunction.

218
00:23:36,707 --> 00:23:40,793
But there are four more across
the North Atlantic showing the same thing.

219
00:23:40,961 --> 00:23:43,171
This is unbelievable.

220
00:23:43,881 --> 00:23:45,590
You predicted it would happen.

221
00:23:45,758 --> 00:23:49,469
Yes, but not in our lifetime.
This is too fast.

222
00:23:49,637 --> 00:23:53,056
There are no forecast models
remotely capable...

223
00:23:53,223 --> 00:23:56,559
...of plotting this scenario, except yours.

224
00:23:56,727 --> 00:23:59,896
My model is a reconstruction
of a prehistoric climate shift.

225
00:24:00,064 --> 00:24:02,190
It's not a forecast model.

226
00:24:02,358 --> 00:24:04,567
It's the closest thing we have.

227
00:24:05,653 --> 00:24:08,404
Nothing like this has ever
happened before.

228
00:24:08,781 --> 00:24:11,824
At least not in the last 10,000 years.

229
00:24:37,726 --> 00:24:43,648
As I predicted yesterday, the swell
off Hurricane Noelani is incredible.

230
00:24:43,816 --> 00:24:48,236
These waves are even bigger
than I imagined. Just take a look.

231
00:24:49,697 --> 00:24:54,450
- Shouldn't you be monitoring the weather?
- This is L.A. What weather?

232
00:24:56,453 --> 00:24:59,122
Wait. What's that noise?

233
00:24:59,289 --> 00:25:01,165
What noise?

234
00:25:12,720 --> 00:25:14,470
Honey, I'll be right back.

235
00:25:16,306 --> 00:25:21,602
The Coast Guard closed the beaches,
as waves have grown too big for...

236
00:25:21,770 --> 00:25:23,604
- L.A. Weather Center.
- It's Tommy.

237
00:25:23,772 --> 00:25:26,566
I'm in the middle of something, Tommy.
What do you need?

238
00:25:27,985 --> 00:25:31,029
There's hail the size of golf balls
coming down here.

239
00:25:31,196 --> 00:25:33,740
A low-pressure system along
the California coast...

240
00:25:33,907 --> 00:25:36,659
...is creating a cyclonic system
across the L.A. Basin.

241
00:25:44,209 --> 00:25:46,377
- Yeah?
- Boss, turn on the Weather Channel.

242
00:25:46,837 --> 00:25:50,548
- I think we have to issue a tornado warning.
- What are you talking about?

243
00:25:51,383 --> 00:25:54,469
Palmdale and Lancaster are reporting
wind speeds in excess...

244
00:25:54,636 --> 00:25:57,972
- Hold on a second.
- Conditions highly unusual for California.

245
00:26:17,993 --> 00:26:20,578
We're building a forecast model,
we need- What?

246
00:26:20,746 --> 00:26:23,998
Priority access to the mainframe
for two days, maybe three.

247
00:26:24,166 --> 00:26:27,001
- Oh, is that it? Anything else?
- We need it immediately.

248
00:26:27,169 --> 00:26:29,212
I would say that
you've lost your mind...

249
00:26:29,379 --> 00:26:32,673
...but you've been this way
for the past 20 years.

250
00:26:32,841 --> 00:26:36,844
- Tom, this is important.
- What's this forecast model you're building?

251
00:26:37,012 --> 00:26:41,682
Janet Tokada, this is Jack Hall.
Janet's a hurricane specialist with NASA.

252
00:26:41,850 --> 00:26:46,479
Jack's a paleoclimatologist, and I have
absolutely no idea what he's up to.

253
00:26:46,897 --> 00:26:49,565
Booker. What's going on here?

254
00:26:49,733 --> 00:26:52,610
They just issued a tornado warning
in Los Angeles.

255
00:26:52,778 --> 00:26:55,863
Breaking news as we prepare to go live
to Los Angeles.

256
00:26:56,031 --> 00:26:59,951
Mixed reports are coming in about some
extreme weather occurring in the area.

257
00:27:00,119 --> 00:27:03,746
Okay, we're now going live
to our Fox affiliate in Los Angeles.

258
00:27:07,417 --> 00:27:11,712
We have live coverage now from our Fox 11
chopper. Are you there, Bart?

259
00:27:11,880 --> 00:27:15,216
Yes, I'm here. These tornados
are forming so fast-

260
00:27:15,384 --> 00:27:18,427
- Bart!
- What? Oh! Oh, my God.

261
00:27:18,595 --> 00:27:21,013
Lisa, are you getting this on camera?

262
00:27:21,181 --> 00:27:23,724
This tornado just erased
the Hollywood sign.

263
00:27:23,892 --> 00:27:27,145
The Hollywood sign is gone.
It's just shredded.

264
00:27:38,949 --> 00:27:41,075
Bart, what can you see? Is anyone hurt?

265
00:27:41,243 --> 00:27:44,120
I wouldn't be surprised.
There is so much damage.

266
00:27:44,329 --> 00:27:47,081
And there are people down there
taking pictures.

267
00:27:47,249 --> 00:27:50,251
Hey, what the hell are you guys doing?
Go for cover!

268
00:27:50,419 --> 00:27:52,920
You can't stay here! Get out of here!

269
00:27:55,465 --> 00:28:00,469
What you're seeing are two actual tornados
striking Los Angeles International Airport.

270
00:28:00,637 --> 00:28:03,931
Wait. It looks like they've joined
and formed one large tornado.

271
00:28:04,099 --> 00:28:05,683
- Tommy!
- Oh, my God!

272
00:28:06,935 --> 00:28:08,895
Holy shit!

273
00:28:09,730 --> 00:28:12,315
I'd like to urge all of our viewers
to stay away...

274
00:28:12,482 --> 00:28:15,943
- Jeff, where are you?
- I'm on Yucca and Vine.

275
00:28:16,111 --> 00:28:19,197
- I'm on my way.
- You're on TV. You're in the middle of it.

276
00:28:19,364 --> 00:28:22,950
God! Oh, my God!

277
00:28:29,041 --> 00:28:31,042
You gotta get out of there, man.

278
00:28:35,505 --> 00:28:38,049
That bus just got dropped
on top of that Porsche!

279
00:28:38,217 --> 00:28:40,468
Oh, my God.
I hope no one was in that car.

280
00:28:40,636 --> 00:28:43,137
For our national audience
just joining us now...

281
00:28:43,305 --> 00:28:46,849
...we are going live to downtown
Los Angeles right now. Tommy?

282
00:28:47,017 --> 00:28:49,977
If you look over there behind me,
that's a tornado.

283
00:28:50,145 --> 00:28:52,563
Yes, a twister in Los Angeles.

284
00:28:52,731 --> 00:28:56,859
It's one of many tornados
that are destroying our city.

285
00:28:57,027 --> 00:29:00,321
There's another one.
That's the Los Angeles skyline.

286
00:29:00,489 --> 00:29:04,158
It's unbelievable! It's huge!
I've never seen anything like it.

287
00:29:16,755 --> 00:29:18,005
What's happening?

288
00:29:18,173 --> 00:29:21,008
It looks like some sort of...

289
00:29:21,176 --> 00:29:26,264
...huge, horrific, terrifying nightmare,
only this is the real thing.

290
00:30:35,208 --> 00:30:37,418
Yes, I'm looking at it right now.

291
00:30:38,086 --> 00:30:39,754
- Yes, it is.
- What's happening?

292
00:30:39,921 --> 00:30:41,339
I'll call you back.

293
00:30:42,341 --> 00:30:46,510
Mr. President, Los Angeles has been
devastated by a series of tornados.

294
00:30:48,472 --> 00:30:52,224
On top of that, the FAA wants your
approval to suspend all air traffic.

295
00:30:52,392 --> 00:30:55,561
- What do you think we should do?
- Until we can figure it out...

296
00:30:55,729 --> 00:30:57,897
...I don't think we have
much choice, sir.

297
00:30:58,065 --> 00:31:01,650
What you're seeing is what's left
of downtown Los Angeles.

298
00:31:01,818 --> 00:31:04,403
Hey, man, I just got off the phone
with my mom.

299
00:31:04,571 --> 00:31:08,532
Excuse me, you guys. I'm really sorry,
but we need to change the channel.

300
00:31:08,950 --> 00:31:13,371
The FAA has grounded
all air traffic in the United States.

301
00:31:13,538 --> 00:31:16,457
Unfortunately, the order came too late
for two planes...

302
00:31:16,625 --> 00:31:19,627
...brought down by severe turbulence
in the Midwest.

303
00:31:19,795 --> 00:31:22,755
- The first flight...
- So much for "one in a billion. "

304
00:31:26,301 --> 00:31:29,512
All right. All right, listen up, everybody.
Listen up, please.

305
00:31:29,679 --> 00:31:32,765
We've got a lot of work to do,
and we don't have much time...

306
00:31:32,933 --> 00:31:36,519
...so let's get started, please. Vorsteen?

307
00:31:37,104 --> 00:31:38,813
All our grid models are worthless.

308
00:31:38,980 --> 00:31:41,941
I don't think grid models
are gonna be a lot of help here.

309
00:31:42,317 --> 00:31:45,569
Canadians report tremendous circulation
moving from the Arctic.

310
00:31:45,737 --> 00:31:48,948
In Siberia, there's a low-pressure system
never before seen.

311
00:31:49,116 --> 00:31:52,076
And Australia just saw
the strongest typhoon ever recorded.

312
00:31:52,244 --> 00:31:55,830
- These things are interconnected?
- We have to consider the possibility.

313
00:31:55,997 --> 00:31:59,959
The only force strong enough to affect
global weather is the sun.

314
00:32:00,419 --> 00:32:02,837
- What's NASA have to say?
- We've already checked.

315
00:32:03,004 --> 00:32:04,797
Solar output is normal.

316
00:32:04,965 --> 00:32:09,135
- What about the North Atlantic Current?
- What about it?

317
00:32:10,178 --> 00:32:14,348
I got a call last night from Professor Rapson
at the Hedland Center.

318
00:32:14,516 --> 00:32:16,851
He thinks the current has changed.

319
00:32:18,270 --> 00:32:20,271
Oh, come on, Jack.
How could that be?

320
00:32:20,772 --> 00:32:23,774
The current depends upon a balance
of salt and freshwater.

321
00:32:23,942 --> 00:32:26,527
- We all know that.
- Yes...

322
00:32:26,695 --> 00:32:30,531
...but no one knows how much freshwater
has been dumped into the ocean...

323
00:32:30,699 --> 00:32:33,200
...because of melting polar ice.

324
00:32:33,535 --> 00:32:37,288
I think we've hit a critical
desalinization point.

325
00:32:39,374 --> 00:32:42,042
It would explain what's driving
this extreme weather.

326
00:32:42,210 --> 00:32:45,045
Hedland had some
pretty convincing data.

327
00:32:45,213 --> 00:32:49,341
They've asked me to feed it into my
paleoclimate model to track the next events.

328
00:32:49,509 --> 00:32:52,887
Are you suggesting these weather
anomalies are gonna continue?

329
00:32:53,346 --> 00:32:55,973
Not just continue. Get worse.

330
00:33:00,103 --> 00:33:04,023
I think we're on the verge
of a major climate shift.

331
00:33:09,070 --> 00:33:11,405
What are you gonna tell
the Administration?

332
00:33:11,573 --> 00:33:15,534
- What do you expect me to tell them?
- The government has to make preparations.

333
00:33:15,702 --> 00:33:18,662
- You have a theory.
- Give me the mainframe. I'll prove it.

334
00:33:18,830 --> 00:33:20,748
No.

335
00:33:23,585 --> 00:33:25,503
You have 48 hours.

336
00:33:26,588 --> 00:33:28,088
- Professor Hall.
- Yes.

337
00:33:28,256 --> 00:33:31,133
- I think your theory may be correct.
- Walk with me.

338
00:33:31,301 --> 00:33:34,762
Just a few weeks ago, I monitored
the strongest hurricane on record.

339
00:33:34,930 --> 00:33:37,640
The hail, the tornados, it all fits.

340
00:33:37,807 --> 00:33:40,434
Can your model factor in
storm scenarios?

341
00:33:40,602 --> 00:33:43,854
- We haven't had the time.
- Well, maybe I can help.

342
00:33:44,022 --> 00:33:45,689
- Welcome aboard.
- Thanks.

343
00:33:45,857 --> 00:33:47,900
Hi, I'm Jason.

344
00:33:48,068 --> 00:33:50,277
- Hi.
- Hi.

345
00:33:52,322 --> 00:33:54,198
Do you have Peter's CT scan results?

346
00:33:54,366 --> 00:33:56,951
Yeah. The treatments
shrunk the tumor 20 percent.

347
00:33:57,118 --> 00:33:59,954
- Is his eyesight better today?
- No. No change.

348
00:34:10,006 --> 00:34:13,717
- Hi, Peter. How are you doing today?
- A little better.

349
00:34:13,885 --> 00:34:15,511
Good.

350
00:34:16,179 --> 00:34:18,305
Let me listen here.

351
00:34:19,599 --> 00:34:20,849
Can you read that?

352
00:34:21,142 --> 00:34:25,437
No, but I remember the story
from the pictures.

353
00:34:25,814 --> 00:34:27,106
You do?

354
00:34:27,357 --> 00:34:29,400
My mother used to read it to me.

355
00:34:29,859 --> 00:34:33,904
She must be very proud of you.
You've been such a brave, big boy.

356
00:34:34,656 --> 00:34:37,616
- Thank you.
- You're welcome.

357
00:34:38,285 --> 00:34:41,161
- Here you go.
- Thank you.

358
00:34:45,625 --> 00:34:49,044
Jack, you've been working
for 24 hours straight.

359
00:34:49,212 --> 00:34:51,964
You're the only one
who hasn't taken a break.

360
00:34:53,258 --> 00:34:55,884
Maybe I'll try to shut my eyes
for a while.

361
00:34:56,136 --> 00:34:58,220
Call me when you get the results.

362
00:35:02,559 --> 00:35:04,643
Frank, is he always so obsessive?

363
00:35:04,811 --> 00:35:06,770
- Yeah.
- Yes.

364
00:35:08,898 --> 00:35:10,316
Does he ever lighten up?

365
00:35:10,483 --> 00:35:12,151
- Not really.
- No.

366
00:35:15,655 --> 00:35:20,075
- How long have you been working together?
- Well, Frank's been working with him...

367
00:35:20,243 --> 00:35:25,664
...since the Stone Age, but I've only
had to endure two years of servitude.

368
00:35:33,340 --> 00:35:34,882
Jack.

369
00:35:35,050 --> 00:35:37,051
Jack, we got the results.

370
00:35:45,477 --> 00:35:47,811
Six to eight months? That can't be.

371
00:35:48,772 --> 00:35:51,607
That time scale isn't in months.
It's in weeks.

372
00:35:55,654 --> 00:36:01,075
Flooding has caused numerous closures,
including the Lincoln and Holland Tunnels.

373
00:36:01,326 --> 00:36:06,080
The plumbing in the school is really old.
With this rain, the sewage got stopped up.

374
00:36:06,247 --> 00:36:07,706
Where are you staying?

375
00:36:07,874 --> 00:36:10,417
They're finding a place for us
with kids here.

376
00:36:10,585 --> 00:36:13,045
You can't get home any sooner
than tomorrow?

377
00:36:13,421 --> 00:36:17,174
Well, look, Dad, I would if I could,
you know. It's just...

378
00:36:18,885 --> 00:36:21,261
This smell is unbearable, Dad.

379
00:36:21,429 --> 00:36:23,389
Stop kidding around! I want you home.

380
00:36:23,556 --> 00:36:25,265
Dad, I'll be on the train.

381
00:36:25,433 --> 00:36:28,519
Do me a favor. Just don't worry about me.
I'll figure it out.

382
00:36:29,479 --> 00:36:32,773
All right, son. I'll see you tomorrow.

383
00:36:35,527 --> 00:36:37,861
Hey, Sam, guess what?

384
00:36:38,071 --> 00:36:40,239
We got a place to stay.

385
00:36:41,282 --> 00:36:42,574
Great.

386
00:36:42,742 --> 00:36:48,122
So far, the terrible weather hasn't hit D.C.,
but local residents aren't taking chances...

387
00:36:48,289 --> 00:36:51,875
...as people stock up for what is already
being billed as...

388
00:36:52,043 --> 00:36:54,545
- ... the worst storm season on record.
- Better be sure.

389
00:36:54,713 --> 00:36:56,839
- My ass is on the line.
- You saw the model.

390
00:36:57,006 --> 00:36:59,383
And I hope to God it's wrong.

391
00:36:59,801 --> 00:37:01,510
- Mr. Vice President.
- Tom.

392
00:37:01,678 --> 00:37:04,346
- You know Professor Hall.
- Yes, we've met.

393
00:37:04,514 --> 00:37:07,766
Professor Hall has some information
I think you should look at.

394
00:37:07,934 --> 00:37:10,769
We just got these results
from our simulation model.

395
00:37:10,937 --> 00:37:14,106
- They explain what's causing this weather.
- I'll read it later.

396
00:37:14,274 --> 00:37:17,568
- I have to meet with the director of FEMA-
- This is very urgent.

397
00:37:17,736 --> 00:37:22,114
Our climate is changing violently. It will
happen over the next six to eight weeks.

398
00:37:22,282 --> 00:37:24,867
You said this wouldn't happen
for another 100 years.

399
00:37:25,034 --> 00:37:27,703
- I was wrong.
- Well, suppose you're wrong this time.

400
00:37:27,871 --> 00:37:31,039
I wish I were, but you're aware
of what's happening everywhere.

401
00:37:31,207 --> 00:37:35,586
We're making all the necessary preparations
for this storm. What more do you expect?

402
00:37:35,754 --> 00:37:39,631
You have to start thinking about
large-scale evacuations right now.

403
00:37:39,799 --> 00:37:41,508
Especially in the Northern states.

404
00:37:41,676 --> 00:37:43,260
- Evacuations?
- Yes.

405
00:37:43,428 --> 00:37:46,889
Have you lost your mind, Hall?
I have to go.

406
00:37:47,974 --> 00:37:52,144
Mr. Vice President! If we don't act now,
it's going to be too late.

407
00:37:56,191 --> 00:37:58,108
Come on, Jack.

408
00:37:58,485 --> 00:38:00,110
Thanks for bringing us here.

409
00:38:00,278 --> 00:38:04,156
I couldn't let you leave New York without
seeing the Natural History Museum.

410
00:38:04,324 --> 00:38:07,201
Of course not. It's a fine collection
of stuffed animals.

411
00:38:07,368 --> 00:38:08,702
Hey, guys, check this out.

412
00:38:09,078 --> 00:38:13,874
"The body of this mammoth was found
perfectly preserved in the Siberian tundra...

413
00:38:14,542 --> 00:38:18,504
...with food still in its mouth
and stomach...

414
00:38:18,838 --> 00:38:23,842
...indicating that it froze instantly
while grazing. "

415
00:38:28,890 --> 00:38:33,060
It's been 24 hours now since the snow
started falling across the British Isles...

416
00:38:33,228 --> 00:38:36,271
...and over Northern Europe.
It shows no signs of letting up.

417
00:38:36,439 --> 00:38:39,233
No, no, no.
You've got to stop worrying. No, no.

418
00:38:39,400 --> 00:38:43,195
It's fine. We've got plenty of supplies.
We're just snowed in.

419
00:38:43,571 --> 00:38:45,989
Yeah. No, it's all right. It's all right.

420
00:38:46,157 --> 00:38:48,200
No, you stay where you are.
I'll be fine.

421
00:38:48,493 --> 00:38:51,245
Yeah, I love you too. Okay, bye.

422
00:38:52,580 --> 00:38:56,542
- Hey. How's Jeanette?
- Oh, fine.

423
00:38:56,709 --> 00:38:58,669
The ferry just landed.

424
00:38:58,837 --> 00:39:00,838
Must be nice in Spain.
Wish I was there.

425
00:39:01,005 --> 00:39:04,550
An elite RAF search-and-rescue team
has been deployed by helicopter...

426
00:39:04,717 --> 00:39:07,386
...to airlift the royal family to safety.

427
00:39:07,720 --> 00:39:11,431
- Yeah, you think they'll come get us?
- Not likely.

428
00:39:12,141 --> 00:39:16,603
Luckily we've got our own genny,
enough tea and biscuits to sink a ship.

429
00:39:16,771 --> 00:39:18,689
Oh, we'll be fine.

430
00:39:18,857 --> 00:39:21,358
As long as the loo
doesn't back up again.

431
00:39:25,280 --> 00:39:28,156
In Ireland, those remaining
in Belfast and Bambridge...

432
00:39:28,324 --> 00:39:32,452
...have been urged to evacuate
to Dublin as soon as possible.

433
00:39:32,620 --> 00:39:35,205
The north continues to receive
the worst of the storm...

434
00:39:35,373 --> 00:39:37,791
...and conditions in the south
are expected to worsen.

435
00:39:41,629 --> 00:39:44,715
We've got zero visibility.
Maintain heading and speed.

436
00:39:44,883 --> 00:39:47,175
- What's our heading?
-035, sir.

437
00:39:47,343 --> 00:39:50,304
Approximately 40 kilometers
from Balmoral Castle.

438
00:39:50,471 --> 00:39:53,056
We've lost visual contact with you.

439
00:40:16,915 --> 00:40:19,958
- This gauge can't be right.
- Wind speed has dropped to zero.

440
00:40:20,126 --> 00:40:23,086
We have vertical-walled
cirrus cloud formations.

441
00:40:23,504 --> 00:40:28,383
- What the hell is going on?
- The bloody fuel lines are starting to freeze.

442
00:40:38,227 --> 00:40:41,980
Port engine pressure is dropping.
The rotor rpm is dropping.

443
00:40:42,148 --> 00:40:45,317
We've got a flameout on
the starboard side as well.

444
00:40:48,780 --> 00:40:50,864
Prepare for crash landing.

445
00:40:52,408 --> 00:40:54,910
Select emergency fuel.

446
00:40:55,453 --> 00:40:57,621
Come on, you bastard!

447
00:40:58,164 --> 00:40:59,998
Come on!

448
00:41:23,314 --> 00:41:26,108
What I'm about to say
is supposed to be confidential.

449
00:41:26,275 --> 00:41:30,028
Several hours ago, three helicopters
went down over Scotland.

450
00:41:30,196 --> 00:41:33,615
They crashed because the fuel
in their lines froze.

451
00:41:34,492 --> 00:41:38,704
- At what temperature does-?
- Negative 150 degrees Fahrenheit.

452
00:41:38,871 --> 00:41:40,163
We had to look it up.

453
00:41:40,331 --> 00:41:42,874
The temperature dropped
phenomenally fast.

454
00:41:43,042 --> 00:41:46,753
On the ground, people froze before
they could get out of their cars even.

455
00:41:46,921 --> 00:41:49,840
Can you get a satellite picture
of Scotland two hours ago?

456
00:41:50,008 --> 00:41:52,175
We've got mountains of data...

457
00:41:52,343 --> 00:41:56,138
...but nowhere near enough computer
power to analyze it. Can you help us?

458
00:41:56,514 --> 00:41:58,515
Send us what you got.
We'll do our best.

459
00:41:58,683 --> 00:42:01,685
Thanks, Jack. Bye for now.

460
00:42:02,645 --> 00:42:06,148
This is Scotland at the time
the temperature dropped.

461
00:42:08,609 --> 00:42:10,902
This thing looks just like a hurricane.

462
00:42:11,070 --> 00:42:14,031
Only hurricanes don't form over land.

463
00:42:21,497 --> 00:42:23,957
- Good evening, sir.
- Hey, Harold.

464
00:42:24,125 --> 00:42:26,835
- Thanks, Victor. See you in the morning.
- Right, sir.

465
00:42:27,003 --> 00:42:28,962
- Terrible weather.
- Tell me about it.

466
00:42:33,509 --> 00:42:35,093
Wow.

467
00:42:36,804 --> 00:42:38,305
You live here?

468
00:42:38,473 --> 00:42:40,307
Just on the weekends.

469
00:42:40,475 --> 00:42:43,185
It's my dad's place.
He's kind of never around, so...

470
00:42:43,352 --> 00:42:44,728
Where is he?

471
00:42:44,896 --> 00:42:48,065
Skiing in Europe with my stepmom.

472
00:42:50,860 --> 00:42:52,611
Is this you and your brother?

473
00:42:52,779 --> 00:42:55,906
Yeah, that's when we took
a bike trip together.

474
00:43:00,161 --> 00:43:03,413
It's been raining like this
for three days now.

475
00:43:13,966 --> 00:43:16,426
Come on, hurry up.

476
00:43:30,274 --> 00:43:31,650
I'm just standing here.

477
00:43:32,360 --> 00:43:33,652
You can't stay here.

478
00:43:34,070 --> 00:43:36,071
I never liked this neighborhood anyway.

479
00:43:36,239 --> 00:43:38,824
It's a mob scene here
at Grand Central Station.

480
00:43:38,991 --> 00:43:44,204
Over half the platforms are flooded and
service has been suspended on all trains.

481
00:43:44,372 --> 00:43:47,457
With planes still grounded
and trains now out of service...

482
00:43:47,625 --> 00:43:49,376
...that's bad news for the...

483
00:43:49,544 --> 00:43:53,630
Victor's coming to pick me up.
You guys want a ride to the train station?

484
00:43:53,798 --> 00:43:55,048
Not anymore.

485
00:43:55,216 --> 00:44:00,637
In Nova Scotia earlier today the ocean
rose by 25 feet in a matter of seconds.

486
00:44:00,805 --> 00:44:04,432
What we have feared for the past
few days has indeed happened.

487
00:44:04,600 --> 00:44:06,726
The cold front moving from the Arctic...

488
00:44:06,894 --> 00:44:09,563
...has created an enormous
storm system in Canada...

489
00:44:09,730 --> 00:44:13,567
...which, incredible as it sounds,
looks more like a tropical hurricane...

490
00:44:13,734 --> 00:44:17,070
I gotta go pick up my little brother.
Do you guys want a ride?

491
00:44:17,238 --> 00:44:20,448
- Where is he?
- He's in a boarding school in Philadelphia.

492
00:44:20,616 --> 00:44:22,117
If this system moves south...

493
00:44:22,285 --> 00:44:26,913
...we could see a wind-driven storm surge
threaten the entire Eastern Seaboard.

494
00:44:28,833 --> 00:44:30,083
Okay, bye.

495
00:44:30,251 --> 00:44:32,794
Victor's stuck in traffic
over on Fifth Avenue.

496
00:44:32,962 --> 00:44:36,131
It'll be easier to head out of town
if we meet him over there.

497
00:44:36,299 --> 00:44:39,134
You mean walk?
No, not in this.

498
00:44:43,014 --> 00:44:44,764
We should take the stairs.

499
00:44:44,932 --> 00:44:47,601
We're on the top floor.

500
00:44:50,563 --> 00:44:52,189
I guess we're walking.

501
00:45:05,328 --> 00:45:07,245
Maybe we should just stay here.

502
00:45:07,413 --> 00:45:08,997
I think the young lady is right.

503
00:45:09,415 --> 00:45:11,917
No. We need to get home.

504
00:45:36,525 --> 00:45:38,485
Hey, Cesar, come here.

505
00:45:39,111 --> 00:45:40,737
What are you doing?

506
00:45:45,826 --> 00:45:48,536
The wolves, they're gone.

507
00:45:48,704 --> 00:45:50,914
Just to give you an idea
of the situation...

508
00:45:51,082 --> 00:45:54,209
...which seems to be becoming worse
with each passing minute:

509
00:45:54,377 --> 00:45:57,963
At the moment, we have flooding
in most parts of the island.

510
00:45:58,130 --> 00:46:01,508
We've got traffic snarl-ups
because the electricity is now out...

511
00:46:01,676 --> 00:46:05,178
...to almost every part of Manhattan.
No traffic signals.

512
00:46:05,346 --> 00:46:07,681
Car accidents, at least 200.

513
00:46:07,848 --> 00:46:12,269
And lower Manhattan, we've been told,
is virtually inaccessible.

514
00:46:33,291 --> 00:46:35,834
Can you call Mom?
Will you please phone her for me?

515
00:46:36,002 --> 00:46:37,794
Hey.

516
00:46:38,421 --> 00:46:39,879
That dog can't come in here.

517
00:46:40,047 --> 00:46:42,549
Come on, man. It's pouring out there.

518
00:46:42,717 --> 00:46:44,884
I don't care. Read the sign.

519
00:46:46,846 --> 00:46:49,639
It's supposed to be a public library.

520
00:46:55,896 --> 00:46:57,939
Come on, guys, this way.

521
00:46:58,524 --> 00:47:00,233
Excuse me. Your bad.

522
00:47:00,401 --> 00:47:03,611
Goddamn 1500-dollar
waterproof raincoat.

523
00:47:03,779 --> 00:47:06,698
- Please, shut up, man.
- There must be rats everywhere.

524
00:47:06,866 --> 00:47:08,366
That's because it's New York.

525
00:47:08,534 --> 00:47:11,202
Hey! Hey!

526
00:47:11,579 --> 00:47:14,164
- Open up, big papa.
- It's out of service.

527
00:47:14,749 --> 00:47:16,458
It's out of service.

528
00:47:16,625 --> 00:47:19,502
- I'll give you $100.
- You don't have to do that.

529
00:47:19,670 --> 00:47:22,339
No, really. $200. I won't have it.

530
00:47:22,506 --> 00:47:25,842
God, I love buses. This is so much fun.
This is gonna be the bomb.

531
00:47:26,761 --> 00:47:28,094
Hello?

532
00:47:28,262 --> 00:47:30,930
- I can't reach my driver. No signal.
- This is insane.

533
00:47:31,098 --> 00:47:33,767
We're not gonna be able to drive.
We should go back.

534
00:47:33,934 --> 00:47:37,312
- Yeah, I vote for that.
- Are you kidding? We have to get higher!

535
00:47:37,730 --> 00:47:39,147
Come on.

536
00:47:39,315 --> 00:47:41,524
Up to the library.

537
00:47:41,692 --> 00:47:44,319
Calm down.

538
00:47:44,487 --> 00:47:46,446
I can't understand what you're saying.

539
00:47:48,908 --> 00:47:51,076
If you stay calm, ma'am,
I'll get you out.

540
00:47:56,665 --> 00:47:59,000
The door is jammed!

541
00:48:00,044 --> 00:48:01,378
I don't speak French!

542
00:48:36,956 --> 00:48:38,248
Hey, where's Laura?

543
00:48:40,334 --> 00:48:44,462
- She was just right there.
- She's right there! Right there, see?

544
00:48:44,630 --> 00:48:46,089
What is she doing?

545
00:48:46,257 --> 00:48:49,008
Tell them to cover their eyes.

546
00:49:12,283 --> 00:49:16,828
There is a wall of water coming towards
New York City. Everybody-

547
00:50:01,540 --> 00:50:03,833
- What?
- Her bag's in the cab. Their passports.

548
00:50:04,001 --> 00:50:06,085
Forget about it.

549
00:50:06,253 --> 00:50:08,421
- I'll get it for her.
- Come on.

550
00:50:14,970 --> 00:50:16,262
Oh, my...

551
00:50:22,394 --> 00:50:30,902
Laura!

552
00:50:31,904 --> 00:50:33,821
- Sam.
- No! Brian, no!

553
00:50:33,989 --> 00:50:36,199
Laura! Laura, look!

554
00:50:36,367 --> 00:50:38,034
Come on. Come!

555
00:50:38,827 --> 00:50:40,453
Come on!

556
00:50:42,915 --> 00:50:44,290
Sam!

557
00:51:24,164 --> 00:51:25,707
Professor.

558
00:51:26,709 --> 00:51:28,126
Thank you.

559
00:51:29,503 --> 00:51:32,839
- Is that Neville's handiwork?
- Neville's way beyond stick figures.

560
00:51:33,007 --> 00:51:34,841
- He's 6 already.
- Oh.

561
00:51:35,009 --> 00:51:37,969
No, this masterpiece belongs
to my second grandson, David.

562
00:51:38,137 --> 00:51:41,306
God. I can't believe Neville's 6 already.

563
00:51:45,144 --> 00:51:47,061
You won't believe how fast they grow.

564
00:51:49,398 --> 00:51:51,232
I've got Jack Hall on the phone.

565
00:51:51,650 --> 00:51:53,818
They've run the data we've sent them.

566
00:51:55,362 --> 00:51:56,904
Here he is.

567
00:51:59,742 --> 00:52:03,202
Jack, were you able to re-create
the thermal cycle?

568
00:52:03,370 --> 00:52:05,246
Yes. The storm's rotation...

569
00:52:05,414 --> 00:52:09,584
...is pulling super-cooled air all the way
down from the upper troposphere.

570
00:52:11,587 --> 00:52:15,590
But shouldn't the air warm up
before it reaches ground level?

571
00:52:15,758 --> 00:52:20,053
It should. But it doesn't.
The air's descending too rapidly.

572
00:52:21,055 --> 00:52:23,598
Is this an isolated incident?

573
00:52:26,143 --> 00:52:28,061
I'm afraid not.

574
00:52:28,228 --> 00:52:31,564
We've located two supercells
in addition to the one over Scotland.

575
00:52:31,732 --> 00:52:33,399
One over Northern Canada...

576
00:52:33,567 --> 00:52:36,569
...and another one over Siberia.

577
00:52:36,737 --> 00:52:39,364
And do we know their projected paths?

578
00:52:39,531 --> 00:52:40,823
Yes.

579
00:52:40,991 --> 00:52:44,243
Our previous estimates of six to
eight weeks weren't even close.

580
00:52:44,411 --> 00:52:47,955
This one storm is going to change
the face of our planet.

581
00:52:48,123 --> 00:52:52,001
Here's a projection of 24 hours out.

582
00:52:56,757 --> 00:52:59,008
This is 48 hours out.

583
00:53:00,761 --> 00:53:03,638
And in seven to 10 days...

584
00:53:06,767 --> 00:53:09,018
When this storm is over...

585
00:53:09,186 --> 00:53:11,979
...we'll be in a new ice age.

586
00:53:16,026 --> 00:53:17,902
My God.

587
00:53:36,130 --> 00:53:38,047
Professor...

588
00:53:38,215 --> 00:53:40,216
...it's time you got out of there.

589
00:53:42,219 --> 00:53:45,388
I'm afraid that time has
come and gone, my friend.

590
00:53:50,436 --> 00:53:52,729
What can we do?

591
00:53:55,941 --> 00:53:58,484
Save as many as you can.

592
00:54:13,375 --> 00:54:15,084
Jack...

593
00:54:15,252 --> 00:54:17,503
...something's happened in New York.

594
00:54:28,056 --> 00:54:30,892
No, the power is out.
I've been in here all day.

595
00:54:31,059 --> 00:54:34,187
- Who needs help?
- Here. Over here.

596
00:54:40,903 --> 00:54:42,570
What?

597
00:54:43,447 --> 00:54:46,699
This is the last one. Enjoy it.

598
00:54:46,867 --> 00:54:48,409
Greedy.

599
00:54:49,620 --> 00:54:51,996
All circuits are busy at this time.

600
00:54:52,164 --> 00:54:54,165
Listen, thanks for coming back for me.

601
00:54:55,959 --> 00:54:57,668
It was really brave.

602
00:55:09,723 --> 00:55:12,141
I guess I better return her bag.

603
00:55:29,409 --> 00:55:30,785
Sam?

604
00:55:31,286 --> 00:55:33,371
Just tell her how you feel.

605
00:55:37,417 --> 00:55:38,918
Yeah.

606
00:55:44,007 --> 00:55:47,677
- Did you reach your little brother yet?
- No, there's still no service.

607
00:55:47,845 --> 00:55:49,887
Damn cell phones.

608
00:55:54,101 --> 00:55:57,395
Excuse me. Are there any
pay phones on the upper floors?

609
00:55:57,563 --> 00:55:59,146
No, no, no.

610
00:55:59,314 --> 00:56:01,357
- There are some on the mezzanine.
- Great.

611
00:56:01,525 --> 00:56:04,569
- But I think it's underwater.
- Where you going? Power's out.

612
00:56:04,736 --> 00:56:07,238
Older payphones draw power
directly from the line.

613
00:56:07,406 --> 00:56:10,950
There will be an emergency meeting
of all NOAA department heads...

614
00:56:11,702 --> 00:56:14,579
Oh, God. I've been trying to reach Sam.

615
00:56:14,746 --> 00:56:16,455
So have I.

616
00:56:17,124 --> 00:56:19,083
I tried to call
and couldn't get you.

617
00:56:19,251 --> 00:56:21,294
It's been a madhouse here. Come on.

618
00:56:26,925 --> 00:56:29,135
Are you sure about this?

619
00:56:39,229 --> 00:56:40,521
It works.

620
00:56:45,944 --> 00:56:47,445
I love that picture.

621
00:56:47,613 --> 00:56:49,822
Yeah, so do I.

622
00:56:50,782 --> 00:56:52,158
Where was that taken?

623
00:56:52,326 --> 00:56:53,618
Miami.

624
00:56:54,077 --> 00:56:56,454
Well, where was I?
I don't remember that trip.

625
00:56:56,622 --> 00:56:59,415
Sam and I went with my sister.
You were in Alaska...

626
00:56:59,583 --> 00:57:02,501
...doing research on your doctorate.

627
00:57:06,298 --> 00:57:09,258
Remember what he was like
when he was that age?

628
00:57:09,760 --> 00:57:11,969
Everything was "one more. "

629
00:57:12,137 --> 00:57:17,058
One more bedtime story. One more ride
on my shoulders. "One more, Daddy. "

630
00:57:18,894 --> 00:57:23,147
Jack. Sam's on the phone. Line four.

631
00:57:23,315 --> 00:57:25,191
- Sam?
- Dad!

632
00:57:25,359 --> 00:57:29,445
- Where are you? Are you all right?
- I'm all right. We're at the Public Library.

633
00:57:29,613 --> 00:57:32,531
- Sam, it's Mom. I'm so happy you're okay.
- Mom.

634
00:57:33,367 --> 00:57:36,827
Can you call Laura and Brian's parents
and tell them we're all right?

635
00:57:36,995 --> 00:57:38,496
Yes, of course.

636
00:57:39,706 --> 00:57:41,916
- Sam, what's that noise?
- Sam?

637
00:57:42,376 --> 00:57:44,126
Sam?

638
00:57:46,338 --> 00:57:48,923
What is going on out there, Dad?

639
00:57:49,091 --> 00:57:51,217
Sam. Sam, listen to me.

640
00:57:51,385 --> 00:57:52,843
Listen very carefully.

641
00:57:53,011 --> 00:57:55,096
Forget what I said about heading south.

642
00:57:55,263 --> 00:57:57,473
It's too late.
The storm is gonna get worse.

643
00:57:58,225 --> 00:58:03,270
It's gonna turn into a massive blizzard with
an eye in the center, like a huge hurricane.

644
00:58:03,730 --> 00:58:06,857
Only the air will be so cold,
you could freeze to death in seconds.

645
00:58:10,696 --> 00:58:11,946
Sam?

646
00:58:12,114 --> 00:58:14,740
- Well, what should we do?
- Listen to me, son.

647
00:58:15,242 --> 00:58:16,909
Do not go outside.

648
00:58:17,077 --> 00:58:19,996
Burn what you can to stay warm,
and try to wait it out.

649
00:58:20,163 --> 00:58:24,166
I will come for you. Do you understand me?
I will come for you.

650
00:58:27,295 --> 00:58:28,546
Sam?

651
00:58:29,214 --> 00:58:31,757
- Sam, come back!
- Sam?

652
00:58:32,384 --> 00:58:34,343
Sam, did you hear me?

653
00:58:34,511 --> 00:58:35,761
Did you hear me?

654
00:58:38,390 --> 00:58:39,849
Sam?

655
00:58:41,018 --> 00:58:44,979
- Tell me he's gonna be okay-
- He's gonna be all right.

656
00:58:45,147 --> 00:58:47,189
He's gonna be all right,
you understand?

657
00:59:14,134 --> 00:59:16,927
I thought you'd drowned.

658
00:59:22,059 --> 00:59:25,478
Let's find some dry clothes for you.
Come on.

659
00:59:31,485 --> 00:59:33,235
Where'd you store the arctic gear?

660
00:59:34,362 --> 00:59:36,197
You can't make it to New York, Jack.

661
00:59:36,364 --> 00:59:38,699
I've walked that far before in the snow.

662
00:59:39,576 --> 00:59:41,327
This is not the same.

663
00:59:41,495 --> 00:59:45,706
Jack, this is not the same.
Lucy, tell him.

664
00:59:48,085 --> 00:59:50,252
I have to do this.

665
00:59:52,422 --> 00:59:54,423
I know.

666
01:00:01,181 --> 01:00:03,182
My hands are shak-

667
01:00:03,350 --> 01:00:06,811
- Shaking.
- That's okay. Here.

668
01:00:06,978 --> 01:00:08,938
Here. Come here.

669
01:00:12,692 --> 01:00:14,527
What are you doing?

670
01:00:14,694 --> 01:00:17,154
I'm using my body heat to warm you.

671
01:00:17,405 --> 01:00:22,535
If we let the blood from your arms and legs
rush back to your heart too quickly...

672
01:00:22,702 --> 01:00:25,246
...your heart could fail.

673
01:00:30,252 --> 01:00:32,753
Where did you learn that?

674
01:00:34,881 --> 01:00:38,759
Some of us were actually
paying attention in health class.

675
01:00:41,179 --> 01:00:43,139
How are you feeling?

676
01:00:45,559 --> 01:00:47,393
Much better.

677
01:00:54,317 --> 01:00:56,735
Frank told me about Sam.

678
01:00:56,903 --> 01:01:01,073
I'm not gonna try to talk you out of going,
but there's something I need first.

679
01:01:01,241 --> 01:01:03,993
You have to explain your results
to the Administration.

680
01:01:04,161 --> 01:01:07,037
- I already tried that, Tom.
- I know. It'll be different.

681
01:01:07,747 --> 01:01:09,874
You're gonna brief the president directly.

682
01:01:12,544 --> 01:01:14,628
- Is that the last of it?
- Pretty much.

683
01:01:14,796 --> 01:01:16,422
Okay.

684
01:01:16,590 --> 01:01:19,592
We also found this radio,
but I don't think it works.

685
01:01:20,010 --> 01:01:21,760
Let me see it.

686
01:01:28,226 --> 01:01:30,019
Buddha, keep quiet.

687
01:01:30,187 --> 01:01:32,897
You ain't even supposed
to be in here anyway.

688
01:02:28,495 --> 01:02:29,870
Come on, guys.

689
01:02:37,003 --> 01:02:38,754
Oh, my God.

690
01:03:01,528 --> 01:03:03,737
The basic rule of storms
is they continue...

691
01:03:03,905 --> 01:03:07,241
...until the imbalance that
created them is corrected.

692
01:03:07,409 --> 01:03:10,202
In this case, we're talking
about a global realignment.

693
01:03:11,329 --> 01:03:14,248
This superstorm will last
seven to 10 days.

694
01:03:14,416 --> 01:03:19,545
When it's over, ice and snow will cover
the entire Northern Hemisphere.

695
01:03:19,713 --> 01:03:22,256
The ice and snow will reflect sunlight.

696
01:03:22,424 --> 01:03:24,550
The Earth's atmosphere will restabilize...

697
01:03:24,718 --> 01:03:28,053
...with an average temperature
close to that of the last Ice Age.

698
01:03:30,390 --> 01:03:32,308
Well, what can we do about this?

699
01:03:33,852 --> 01:03:35,769
Head as far south as possible.

700
01:03:36,521 --> 01:03:38,105
That is not amusing, professor.

701
01:03:38,481 --> 01:03:40,566
Where do you suggest they go?

702
01:03:40,734 --> 01:03:43,152
The farther south they go,
the safer they'll be.

703
01:03:43,320 --> 01:03:47,990
Texas. Parts of Florida that aren't
flooded. Mexico would be best.

704
01:03:48,158 --> 01:03:51,744
Mexico? Maybe you should stick
to science and leave policy to us.

705
01:03:51,911 --> 01:03:53,454
Well, we tried that approach.

706
01:03:53,621 --> 01:03:57,666
You didn't want to hear about the science
when it could have made a difference.

707
01:03:59,502 --> 01:04:01,628
What exactly are you proposing, professor?

708
01:04:08,094 --> 01:04:13,265
Evacuate everyone south of that line.

709
01:04:19,272 --> 01:04:21,273
What about the people in the North?

710
01:04:23,610 --> 01:04:25,944
I'm afraid it's too late for them.

711
01:04:26,112 --> 01:04:28,280
If they go outside...

712
01:04:28,448 --> 01:04:30,407
...the storm will kill them.

713
01:04:33,703 --> 01:04:35,496
At this point...

714
01:04:36,581 --> 01:04:39,333
...their best chance is to stay inside.

715
01:04:40,460 --> 01:04:42,586
Try to ride it out.

716
01:04:43,338 --> 01:04:44,797
Pray.

717
01:04:49,886 --> 01:04:51,428
What do you think he'll do?

718
01:04:51,596 --> 01:04:53,472
I don't know.

719
01:04:53,640 --> 01:04:54,932
Jack...

720
01:04:55,809 --> 01:04:57,226
...thanks...

721
01:04:57,394 --> 01:04:58,769
...and good luck.

722
01:04:58,937 --> 01:05:00,646
You too.

723
01:05:02,732 --> 01:05:04,817
We're all gonna need it.

724
01:05:07,404 --> 01:05:12,491
We can't evacuate half the country because
one scientist thinks the climate is shifting.

725
01:05:12,659 --> 01:05:16,578
- Every minute we delay costs lives.
- What about the other half of the country?

726
01:05:16,746 --> 01:05:19,039
If Professor Hall is right
about this storm...

727
01:05:19,207 --> 01:05:21,667
...sending troops north will
create more victims.

728
01:05:21,835 --> 01:05:24,211
We need to save the people
we can right now.

729
01:05:24,379 --> 01:05:27,464
We take the same approach in triage
on the battlefield.

730
01:05:27,632 --> 01:05:30,676
Sometimes it's necessary
to make difficult choices-

731
01:05:30,844 --> 01:05:33,720
I don't accept abandoning
half the country is necessary.

732
01:05:33,888 --> 01:05:36,223
Maybe if you listened sooner,
it wouldn't be.

733
01:05:36,391 --> 01:05:38,851
Bullshit. It's easy for him
to suggest this plan.

734
01:05:39,018 --> 01:05:42,354
- He's safely here in Washington.
- His son is in Manhattan.

735
01:05:45,859 --> 01:05:49,236
I thought you should know before
you start questioning his motives.

736
01:05:51,739 --> 01:05:53,490
We're going to follow Hall's plan.

737
01:05:55,869 --> 01:05:57,286
- General.
- Sir?

738
01:05:57,454 --> 01:06:00,789
Give the order for the National Guard
to evacuate Southern states.

739
01:06:00,957 --> 01:06:02,583
Yes, sir.

740
01:06:17,098 --> 01:06:18,724
Vivian...

741
01:06:18,892 --> 01:06:20,726
...get me my wife.

742
01:06:39,662 --> 01:06:43,123
Sorry, mates,
but we're just about out of petrol.

743
01:06:46,419 --> 01:06:47,836
Hey.

744
01:06:48,379 --> 01:06:49,963
Is there any chance...

745
01:06:50,590 --> 01:06:52,049
...that it'll run...

746
01:06:53,301 --> 01:06:55,219
...on this?

747
01:06:55,386 --> 01:06:56,970
Are you mad?

748
01:06:57,138 --> 01:06:59,515
That's a 12-year-old Scotch.

749
01:07:21,287 --> 01:07:22,579
Gentlemen.

750
01:07:23,498 --> 01:07:25,666
To England.

751
01:07:26,543 --> 01:07:28,418
To mankind.

752
01:07:30,463 --> 01:07:32,339
To Manchester United.

753
01:07:40,181 --> 01:07:41,473
I just...

754
01:07:41,641 --> 01:07:45,602
I just wish I could have seen
him grow up. You know?

755
01:07:49,274 --> 01:07:52,109
The important thing is he will grow up.

756
01:07:57,115 --> 01:07:58,907
Amen.

757
01:08:15,466 --> 01:08:18,218
Maybe you should have somebody
help you with that.

758
01:08:18,845 --> 01:08:21,680
Sir, I am president of the Electronics Club...

759
01:08:21,848 --> 01:08:24,099
...the Math Club and the Chess Club.

760
01:08:24,267 --> 01:08:27,352
Now, if there's a bigger nerd in here,
please, point him out.

761
01:08:29,689 --> 01:08:32,649
I'll just leave you alone
to work on it, okay?

762
01:08:33,901 --> 01:08:36,945
Come on, Buddha, come on.
Do your business.

763
01:08:37,113 --> 01:08:39,906
Look, there's nobody around.

764
01:08:40,074 --> 01:08:42,951
You know what? I'll turn around.
I won't look. I promise.

765
01:08:47,165 --> 01:08:48,832
What?

766
01:08:55,548 --> 01:08:57,382
Hey, man, there's people out there.

767
01:08:57,800 --> 01:08:59,092
I was walking my dog.

768
01:08:59,260 --> 01:09:03,305
There's hundreds of them.
They're walking on the snow.

769
01:09:11,272 --> 01:09:12,814
Where they all going?

770
01:09:12,982 --> 01:09:15,859
They're getting out of the city
before it's too late.

771
01:09:19,030 --> 01:09:21,990
All right, everybody. Quiet down.

772
01:09:22,158 --> 01:09:25,118
When was the last time anyone
got a signal on a cell phone?

773
01:09:25,286 --> 01:09:27,913
I got through to my cousin
in Memphis an hour ago.

774
01:09:28,081 --> 01:09:29,915
They're being evacuated to the South.

775
01:09:30,083 --> 01:09:31,625
We should get moving too.

776
01:09:31,793 --> 01:09:34,419
The water is frozen over
enough to walk on.

777
01:09:34,587 --> 01:09:37,839
We should get going before
the snow gets too deep.

778
01:09:38,007 --> 01:09:41,635
Everyone, wrap yourselves up
as fast as you can.

779
01:09:41,803 --> 01:09:43,261
We shouldn't go.

780
01:09:43,429 --> 01:09:45,263
Why not, Sam? Everyone's leaving.

781
01:09:45,431 --> 01:09:48,141
When I talked to my dad,
he told me to stay inside.

782
01:09:48,309 --> 01:09:50,060
The storm will kill anybody in it.

783
01:09:50,228 --> 01:09:52,979
- Then you have to say something.
- I know.

784
01:09:57,110 --> 01:09:59,611
Excuse me, sir, you're making a mistake.

785
01:09:59,779 --> 01:10:02,739
What? Listen, we're all scared,
but we've got no choice.

786
01:10:02,907 --> 01:10:04,491
- That's not it.
- Get ready to go.

787
01:10:04,659 --> 01:10:08,120
If these people go outside,
they will freeze to death!

788
01:10:08,996 --> 01:10:10,789
Okay, what is this nonsense?

789
01:10:10,957 --> 01:10:14,626
It's not nonsense. All right?
Look, this storm is gonna get worse.

790
01:10:14,794 --> 01:10:17,212
The people caught outside
will freeze to death.

791
01:10:17,380 --> 01:10:20,132
- Where are you getting this?
- My dad's a climatologist.

792
01:10:20,299 --> 01:10:22,968
- He works for the government.
- So, what do we do?

793
01:10:23,136 --> 01:10:25,804
We stay inside, we keep warm,
and we wait it out.

794
01:10:25,972 --> 01:10:28,098
The snow is getting deeper
by the minute.

795
01:10:28,266 --> 01:10:30,809
We'd be trapped here
without food, supplies...

796
01:10:30,977 --> 01:10:33,103
- It's a risk, yeah-
- An unnecessary risk.

797
01:10:33,271 --> 01:10:34,521
No, no. It's not.

798
01:10:34,689 --> 01:10:37,107
We've wasted enough time talking.
Come on.

799
01:10:37,275 --> 01:10:38,608
Let's go.

800
01:10:38,776 --> 01:10:42,154
- Look, look. Just look for a second.
- Come on, everybody-

801
01:10:42,321 --> 01:10:45,741
This storm is gonna get bad.
It's gonna get really, really bad.

802
01:10:45,908 --> 01:10:48,034
You're not gonna be able to survive it.

803
01:10:49,328 --> 01:10:50,620
Believe me.

804
01:10:50,788 --> 01:10:55,083
Sir, please just stay. Just stay.

805
01:10:55,918 --> 01:10:58,086
Just don't...

806
01:11:27,200 --> 01:11:30,327
She's set to go.
Come on, straight to the back, guys.

807
01:11:30,495 --> 01:11:32,537
It'll be impossible to reach each other.

808
01:11:32,705 --> 01:11:35,373
Leave a message for me
at the embassy in Mexico City.

809
01:11:35,541 --> 01:11:36,792
Okay, I will.

810
01:11:38,419 --> 01:11:39,920
I love you.

811
01:11:43,299 --> 01:11:45,717
I love you, Jack.

812
01:11:46,552 --> 01:11:49,429
Tell Sam I love him so much.

813
01:11:50,932 --> 01:11:52,891
God be with you.

814
01:12:00,066 --> 01:12:02,067
- Dr. Hall?
- Yeah.

815
01:12:02,235 --> 01:12:04,611
We haven't been able
to reach Peter's parents.

816
01:12:04,779 --> 01:12:08,406
- I'm gonna try later, okay?
- Okay. Thank you.

817
01:12:15,748 --> 01:12:17,582
I got it.

818
01:12:18,584 --> 01:12:21,211
You're supposed to be on a bus
heading south.

819
01:12:21,379 --> 01:12:25,882
I've been watching your back for 20 years.
You think I'd let you go alone?

820
01:12:26,050 --> 01:12:29,261
And all these years I thought
I was watching your back.

821
01:12:29,428 --> 01:12:31,221
Where are the keys?

822
01:12:31,389 --> 01:12:33,139
In the truck.

823
01:12:33,724 --> 01:12:36,101
Where do you think you're going?

824
01:12:36,269 --> 01:12:38,728
Neither one of you can navigate
worth a damn.

825
01:12:38,896 --> 01:12:40,897
Without me, you'll end up in Cleveland.

826
01:12:43,109 --> 01:12:46,319
I'll try to give you updates on
the storm as it heads your way.

827
01:12:46,863 --> 01:12:48,780
Good luck, Jack.

828
01:13:02,545 --> 01:13:05,255
Breaking news from
the U.S.- Mexico border.

829
01:13:05,423 --> 01:13:08,800
Just half an hour ago,
Mexican officials closed the border...

830
01:13:08,968 --> 01:13:12,637
...in the light of so many U.S. refugees
who are fleeing south...

831
01:13:12,805 --> 01:13:15,015
...in the wake of the approaching storm.

832
01:13:15,182 --> 01:13:18,184
These people came in anticipation
of crossing into Mexico.

833
01:13:18,352 --> 01:13:20,562
Instead, they've been met
with closed gates.

834
01:13:20,730 --> 01:13:24,024
And now, in a dramatic reversal
of illegal immigration...

835
01:13:24,191 --> 01:13:27,777
...thousands of people are crossing
the Rio Grande into Mexico.

836
01:13:27,945 --> 01:13:31,656
The scene unfolding here behind me
is one of desperation and frustration.

837
01:13:31,824 --> 01:13:35,327
People have abandoned their cars,
grabbed their belongings...

838
01:13:35,494 --> 01:13:40,081
...and they are wading across the river
illegally into Mexico.

839
01:13:42,501 --> 01:13:44,085
Here it is.

840
01:13:45,588 --> 01:13:48,965
This fireplace probably hasn't
been used in about 100 years.

841
01:13:51,677 --> 01:13:53,345
All right.

842
01:13:59,894 --> 01:14:02,687
- What are you doing?
- What did you think we would burn?

843
01:14:03,022 --> 01:14:06,191
- You can't burn books.
- No, absolutely not.

844
01:14:06,359 --> 01:14:08,109
You want to freeze to death?

845
01:14:11,197 --> 01:14:13,281
I'll go get some more.

846
01:14:13,449 --> 01:14:14,908
I'll help you.

847
01:14:15,368 --> 01:14:17,494
I'm going with them.

848
01:14:18,788 --> 01:14:21,247
Okay, do you have a cafeteria
or a lunchroom?

849
01:14:21,415 --> 01:14:24,250
Just an employees' lounge
with a few vending machines.

850
01:14:32,176 --> 01:14:34,761
We're not gonna last long
on M&M's and potato chips.

851
01:14:34,929 --> 01:14:37,055
What about the garbage cans?

852
01:14:37,223 --> 01:14:39,808
There's always something
to eat in the garbage.

853
01:14:43,896 --> 01:14:46,564
Friedrich Nietzsche?
We can't burn Nietzsche.

854
01:14:46,732 --> 01:14:49,734
He was the most important thinker
of the 19th century.

855
01:14:49,902 --> 01:14:52,904
Please. He was a chauvinist pig
in love with his sister.

856
01:14:53,114 --> 01:14:54,698
He was not a chauvinist pig.

857
01:14:55,199 --> 01:14:57,200
But he was in love with his sister.

858
01:14:57,368 --> 01:14:59,035
Excuse me? You guys?

859
01:14:59,203 --> 01:15:03,373
Yeah, there's a whole section on tax law
down here that we can burn.

860
01:15:03,541 --> 01:15:06,918
After hours of uncertainty,
the traffic is now moving smoothly...

861
01:15:07,086 --> 01:15:09,295
...from the United States into Mexico.

862
01:15:09,463 --> 01:15:13,341
This is only possible because the president
was able to negotiate a deal...

863
01:15:13,509 --> 01:15:17,929
...to forgive all Latin American debt
in exchange for opening the border.

864
01:15:18,097 --> 01:15:22,267
Now, multitudes of American families
are feeling a rush of relief...

865
01:15:25,938 --> 01:15:27,605
Lucy?

866
01:15:33,529 --> 01:15:35,030
Is Peter's ambulance here?

867
01:15:35,197 --> 01:15:36,865
No.

868
01:15:42,079 --> 01:15:44,539
- Why?
- They've all gone.

869
01:15:44,707 --> 01:15:46,291
- What?
- In the confusion...

870
01:15:46,459 --> 01:15:50,128
...I don't know what happened.
People started to panic, and they left...

871
01:15:50,296 --> 01:15:52,589
- God.
- There's a policeman with a snowplow.

872
01:15:52,757 --> 01:15:55,175
- He's waiting outside.
- God.

873
01:15:55,342 --> 01:15:57,844
Peter can't be moved in anything
but an ambulance.

874
01:15:58,637 --> 01:16:02,891
I called and left a message
for the county ambulance service.

875
01:16:10,566 --> 01:16:12,108
Okay.

876
01:16:14,403 --> 01:16:16,780
Okay, you should go,
and I'll stay and wait.

877
01:16:17,323 --> 01:16:18,615
Lucy, no.

878
01:16:18,783 --> 01:16:20,617
- No.
- No, you should go.

879
01:16:20,785 --> 01:16:22,702
He's not gonna wait forever.

880
01:16:22,870 --> 01:16:24,788
It's okay.

881
01:16:27,333 --> 01:16:29,334
I'm sorry.

882
01:16:30,086 --> 01:16:33,004
- It's okay.
- All right.

883
01:17:01,367 --> 01:17:02,951
You all right?

884
01:17:03,119 --> 01:17:06,121
Yeah, I'm fine. I just-
I cut my leg the other day.

885
01:17:10,251 --> 01:17:11,543
Did you get a signal?

886
01:17:11,710 --> 01:17:13,837
Yeah, for a minute.

887
01:17:14,004 --> 01:17:15,296
And?

888
01:17:19,260 --> 01:17:21,469
Man, this storm is everywhere.

889
01:17:21,637 --> 01:17:24,055
It's hit the entire Northern Hemisphere.

890
01:17:24,723 --> 01:17:27,308
Europe is buried under
15 feet of snow...

891
01:17:27,476 --> 01:17:30,228
...and they say it's gonna get
just as bad here.

892
01:17:30,771 --> 01:17:33,565
I mean, I don't think your dad's
gonna make it.

893
01:17:37,403 --> 01:17:38,987
No, he'll make it.

894
01:17:43,742 --> 01:17:45,577
He'll make it.

895
01:17:50,166 --> 01:17:53,001
The White House has ordered
a national disaster alert...

896
01:17:53,169 --> 01:17:54,586
...for all Northern states.

897
01:17:54,753 --> 01:17:57,463
Continued exposure to
the approaching storm is deadly.

898
01:17:57,631 --> 01:18:01,634
Remain indoors and take all measures to
stay warm, burning any fuel sources...

899
01:18:01,802 --> 01:18:05,513
...until the storm is over. Roads are
impassable across New York State...

900
01:18:05,681 --> 01:18:07,974
...and as far south as Pennsylvania...

901
01:18:08,142 --> 01:18:10,101
Where are we?

902
01:18:12,104 --> 01:18:14,981
Looks like we're just north
of Philadelphia.

903
01:18:18,277 --> 01:18:19,903
Look out!

904
01:18:24,241 --> 01:18:25,992
You guys okay?

905
01:18:26,160 --> 01:18:27,535
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

906
01:18:33,626 --> 01:18:36,628
- Once again, roads across the...
- Sorry, boss.

907
01:18:37,546 --> 01:18:39,964
Unpack the snowshoes.

908
01:18:40,132 --> 01:18:42,050
We're walking from here.

909
01:18:43,636 --> 01:18:46,471
Come on, ma'am.
Let's get up and get going.

910
01:18:47,640 --> 01:18:51,309
We've only made it to Brooklyn.
Maybe we should just turn back.

911
01:18:51,477 --> 01:18:55,104
Why? Half the city's frozen under water.
There's nothing to go back to.

912
01:18:55,272 --> 01:18:56,814
Time to get up and keep moving.

913
01:18:56,982 --> 01:18:59,817
What we should've done
is stayed in the library.

914
01:19:01,445 --> 01:19:04,155
Okay, guys, let's get moving.

915
01:19:13,707 --> 01:19:15,667
Wake up.

916
01:19:33,269 --> 01:19:34,727
What are you doing?

917
01:19:34,895 --> 01:19:36,646
Insulating.

918
01:19:36,814 --> 01:19:39,274
Newspaper's best, but this'll do.

919
01:19:39,566 --> 01:19:43,444
You know, you spend some years on the
streets and you learn how to keep warm.

920
01:19:44,530 --> 01:19:46,364
Hey.

921
01:19:51,620 --> 01:19:53,371
Thanks.

922
01:19:57,126 --> 01:19:59,085
I got one.

923
01:19:59,253 --> 01:20:01,337
Your favorite vacation.

924
01:20:03,048 --> 01:20:05,008
Besides this one?

925
01:20:05,968 --> 01:20:07,260
All right.

926
01:20:07,428 --> 01:20:11,681
I went to Greenland with my dad on one
of his research trips a few years ago...

927
01:20:14,059 --> 01:20:18,563
...and the ship broke down,
and we got stuck.

928
01:20:19,648 --> 01:20:22,233
And it rained constantly.

929
01:20:24,069 --> 01:20:26,404
That sounds really boring.

930
01:20:27,573 --> 01:20:30,074
But actually really nice, you know?

931
01:20:30,242 --> 01:20:33,619
Just me and my dad
hanging out for 10 days.

932
01:21:46,276 --> 01:21:47,985
Frank!

933
01:21:48,320 --> 01:21:49,987
Are you all right?

934
01:21:50,155 --> 01:21:51,364
I'm fine.

935
01:21:53,117 --> 01:21:55,827
I just dropped in to do a little shopping.

936
01:21:57,162 --> 01:21:58,704
I gotta lose this sled.

937
01:22:14,972 --> 01:22:17,807
Hold on! We'll pull you up.

938
01:22:20,227 --> 01:22:23,229
Jason! You've got to support
Frank's weight.

939
01:22:23,397 --> 01:22:26,190
Okay, I'll try.

940
01:22:32,531 --> 01:22:35,199
Okay, I think I have his weight now.

941
01:22:35,367 --> 01:22:37,076
I'm coming to you.

942
01:22:49,798 --> 01:22:52,383
The glass is breaking!

943
01:22:54,553 --> 01:22:56,053
Too much weight.

944
01:22:59,933 --> 01:23:01,476
It's not gonna hold.

945
01:23:11,278 --> 01:23:12,528
No. No.

946
01:23:12,946 --> 01:23:15,865
Frank. I can get you out.

947
01:23:18,243 --> 01:23:19,744
Don't do it!

948
01:23:19,912 --> 01:23:22,288
- No, Frank, no!
- Frank, no!

949
01:23:23,957 --> 01:23:25,833
Frank!

950
01:24:09,336 --> 01:24:11,254
What have you got there?

951
01:24:12,130 --> 01:24:14,549
A Gutenberg Bible.

952
01:24:14,758 --> 01:24:17,051
It was in the rare-books room.

953
01:24:18,345 --> 01:24:20,054
You think God's gonna save you?

954
01:24:20,806 --> 01:24:23,599
No. I don't believe in God.

955
01:24:24,268 --> 01:24:26,936
You're holding on to that Bible
pretty tight.

956
01:24:27,104 --> 01:24:29,105
I'm protecting it.

957
01:24:32,609 --> 01:24:36,112
This Bible is the first book ever printed.

958
01:24:36,947 --> 01:24:41,909
It represents the dawn
of the age of reason.

959
01:24:42,286 --> 01:24:46,330
As far as I'm concerned, the written word
is mankind's greatest achievement.

960
01:24:46,665 --> 01:24:48,207
You can laugh.

961
01:24:49,167 --> 01:24:52,336
But if Western civilization is finished...

962
01:24:52,963 --> 01:24:56,966
...I'm gonna save
at least one little piece of it.

963
01:25:33,712 --> 01:25:35,838
We heard somebody was left behind.

964
01:25:36,757 --> 01:25:40,468
- We brought an ambulance.
- Thank God.

965
01:25:42,929 --> 01:25:45,681
Thank you so much for coming.

966
01:26:06,578 --> 01:26:08,120
Hey.

967
01:26:09,039 --> 01:26:11,248
Hey, are you all right?

968
01:26:12,459 --> 01:26:14,669
You look like you have a fever
or something.

969
01:26:14,836 --> 01:26:17,963
I'm fine. I just can't sleep.

970
01:26:20,884 --> 01:26:26,430
My mind keeps going over
all those worthless decathlon facts.

971
01:26:28,016 --> 01:26:30,309
- It's pretty stupid, huh?
- No, it's all right.

972
01:26:30,477 --> 01:26:33,979
I guess you just haven't had time
to adjust yet.

973
01:26:35,774 --> 01:26:38,359
How am I supposed to adjust, Sam?

974
01:26:38,944 --> 01:26:42,863
Everything I've ever cared about,
everything I've worked for...

975
01:26:43,407 --> 01:26:47,034
...has all been preparation
for a future that no longer exists.

976
01:26:50,497 --> 01:26:54,542
I know you always thought I took
the competition too seriously.

977
01:26:55,836 --> 01:26:57,670
You were right.

978
01:26:58,296 --> 01:27:01,257
- It was all for nothing.
- No, no. I-

979
01:27:02,175 --> 01:27:06,470
No, I just- I just said that
to avoid admitting the truth.

980
01:27:07,848 --> 01:27:09,515
Truth about what?

981
01:27:13,562 --> 01:27:16,939
About why I joined the team.

982
01:27:22,070 --> 01:27:24,238
I joined it because of you.

983
01:27:37,335 --> 01:27:38,794
Hey.

984
01:28:04,571 --> 01:28:07,865
I know.

985
01:28:08,033 --> 01:28:10,159
- Mr. President.
- All right.

986
01:28:10,869 --> 01:28:13,245
I'm sorry, sir, we can't wait any longer.

987
01:28:13,830 --> 01:28:15,831
We're the last ones.

988
01:28:16,708 --> 01:28:18,292
All right.

989
01:28:33,308 --> 01:28:35,059
Mexicali Control, this is Delta 26.

990
01:28:36,645 --> 01:28:38,646
We've got an urgent message
for high command.

991
01:28:38,814 --> 01:28:41,023
Request priority for landing zone one.

992
01:28:41,191 --> 01:28:45,152
Roger that, Delta 26.
Transport will be waiting.

993
01:28:50,242 --> 01:28:51,575
- Sir.
- Thank you, corporal.

994
01:28:51,743 --> 01:28:53,953
- Sorry for the delay, sir.
- Carry on.

995
01:29:06,758 --> 01:29:08,592
Madam Secretary.

996
01:29:18,144 --> 01:29:19,770
Raymond.

997
01:29:20,730 --> 01:29:23,232
The president's motorcade
got caught in the storm.

998
01:29:28,280 --> 01:29:29,905
They didn't make it.

999
01:29:36,288 --> 01:29:38,789
And she wouldn't wake up
this morning. I-

1000
01:29:38,957 --> 01:29:41,417
She only had a fever.
I don't understand.

1001
01:29:41,585 --> 01:29:43,752
She's awfully pale.

1002
01:29:43,962 --> 01:29:46,463
None of us has had anything
real to eat in days.

1003
01:29:46,631 --> 01:29:49,633
- It's hypothermia.
- But how can it be hypothermia?

1004
01:29:49,801 --> 01:29:51,844
- We've all been in-
- Maybe it's the flu.

1005
01:29:52,012 --> 01:29:54,305
No, no, it's not the flu.

1006
01:29:55,557 --> 01:29:56,807
And how do you know?

1007
01:29:57,267 --> 01:30:00,853
Books can be good for things other
than burning. What are her symptoms?

1008
01:30:01,021 --> 01:30:04,773
I told you that she has a fever and her-
She's got a really cold sweat.

1009
01:30:04,941 --> 01:30:06,692
How's her pulse?

1010
01:30:07,152 --> 01:30:09,528
- It's really fast.
- Does she have any injuries?

1011
01:30:09,696 --> 01:30:12,239
Like a cut that might have
gotten infected?

1012
01:30:12,407 --> 01:30:15,910
She was complaining about a cut
on her leg a few days ago.

1013
01:30:16,453 --> 01:30:19,288
I didn't think anything of it.

1014
01:30:23,752 --> 01:30:25,461
Oh, my gosh.

1015
01:30:26,671 --> 01:30:29,840
That's blood poisoning. Septicemia.

1016
01:30:30,008 --> 01:30:32,134
She could go into septic shock.

1017
01:30:32,302 --> 01:30:34,386
I've seen that before.
That can get bad.

1018
01:30:34,554 --> 01:30:36,597
She needs a massive dose
of penicillin...

1019
01:30:36,765 --> 01:30:40,351
...or a broad-spectrum
antibiotic immediately, or...

1020
01:30:46,149 --> 01:30:47,733
Or what?

1021
01:31:07,545 --> 01:31:09,880
I've never seen anything like it.

1022
01:31:10,048 --> 01:31:13,717
There's no point of reference.
All I can see is cloud cover.

1023
01:31:13,885 --> 01:31:15,386
What are you doing?

1024
01:31:15,553 --> 01:31:17,888
Taking infrared image of thermal currents.

1025
01:31:18,056 --> 01:31:22,768
Send to Houston, Korolev,
your weather service.

1026
01:31:22,936 --> 01:31:24,561
I'll help you.

1027
01:31:25,605 --> 01:31:28,065
Tom? Tom, wake up.

1028
01:31:28,233 --> 01:31:32,569
I just received some satellite images from
the space station. You better take a look.

1029
01:31:44,124 --> 01:31:45,666
How big is this thing?

1030
01:31:45,834 --> 01:31:47,876
The vortex is 50 miles in diameter.

1031
01:31:48,044 --> 01:31:51,714
And growing. The two cells
over Europe and Asia are even bigger.

1032
01:31:51,881 --> 01:31:53,173
Good God.

1033
01:31:53,341 --> 01:31:56,260
This one's gonna hit New York
inside an hour.

1034
01:32:12,777 --> 01:32:14,278
Jason!

1035
01:32:14,446 --> 01:32:16,071
Are you all right?

1036
01:32:16,531 --> 01:32:18,323
Jason!

1037
01:32:34,299 --> 01:32:37,593
- What are you doing?
- There's gotta be medicine on that ship.

1038
01:32:38,136 --> 01:32:41,305
I thought you said it was too dangerous
to go outside.

1039
01:32:41,473 --> 01:32:42,806
I know I did.

1040
01:32:49,105 --> 01:32:51,482
Where did you find those chairs?

1041
01:32:52,817 --> 01:32:54,193
Why?

1042
01:32:55,070 --> 01:32:56,612
I'm going with you.

1043
01:32:58,114 --> 01:32:59,573
Me too.

1044
01:34:03,054 --> 01:34:04,972
Come on, damn it!

1045
01:34:53,646 --> 01:34:56,106
- It's all in Russian. I-
- I know.

1046
01:34:56,482 --> 01:34:59,735
- Hey, hey, hey, guys. I found it.
- What? How do you know?

1047
01:35:00,236 --> 01:35:02,529
Because it says "penicillin"
on the bottle.

1048
01:35:07,911 --> 01:35:09,620
Hey, wait. This is the mess hall.

1049
01:35:09,787 --> 01:35:12,122
- We should find some food.
- We don't have time.

1050
01:35:12,290 --> 01:35:16,293
Listen, none of us are gonna survive much
longer without food. Including Laura.

1051
01:35:17,587 --> 01:35:18,879
Okay.

1052
01:35:23,760 --> 01:35:26,345
- Sam, over here.
- What?

1053
01:35:26,888 --> 01:35:28,597
Bingo.

1054
01:35:39,651 --> 01:35:41,276
Brian?

1055
01:35:41,986 --> 01:35:44,655
- I'm okay.
- What happened?

1056
01:35:45,657 --> 01:35:47,741
All I did was open up the cupboard.

1057
01:35:48,326 --> 01:35:49,660
- Well, we can use it.
- For what?

1058
01:35:49,827 --> 01:35:51,078
Put food in it.

1059
01:36:04,008 --> 01:36:05,968
Pull him in, Brian!

1060
01:36:08,513 --> 01:36:10,264
You all right?

1061
01:36:14,060 --> 01:36:15,852
My leg.

1062
01:36:19,691 --> 01:36:21,650
Here, use this.

1063
01:36:29,284 --> 01:36:31,243
It should be over New York by now.

1064
01:36:32,120 --> 01:36:36,623
Satellite readings are showing a temperature
drop of 10 degrees per second.

1065
01:36:52,223 --> 01:36:53,598
You guys...

1066
01:36:53,766 --> 01:36:55,851
...I think we're in the eye of it.

1067
01:36:56,936 --> 01:36:58,812
We gotta get back right now.

1068
01:37:00,231 --> 01:37:04,359
Look, I'm gonna go outside, and I'm gonna
lure the wolves out of the room.

1069
01:37:04,527 --> 01:37:06,862
When they leave, you lock the door.

1070
01:37:07,405 --> 01:37:08,655
Good luck.

1071
01:38:14,639 --> 01:38:17,224
- Brian, hurry.
- I need something.

1072
01:38:17,975 --> 01:38:19,893
Come on, hurry!

1073
01:38:29,654 --> 01:38:31,822
Brian! Brian, open the door!

1074
01:38:36,619 --> 01:38:38,495
We have to get out of here now.

1075
01:38:38,663 --> 01:38:40,831
Brian, get the supplies.

1076
01:39:38,306 --> 01:39:39,931
Oh, shit. Let's go!

1077
01:39:40,600 --> 01:39:41,850
Pull, Brian!

1078
01:40:18,095 --> 01:40:20,180
Take the medicine to Laura.

1079
01:40:51,671 --> 01:40:54,631
We're almost there. Come on!

1080
01:40:55,716 --> 01:40:58,593
Brian! Close the door!

1081
01:41:09,772 --> 01:41:12,107
Don't let the fire go out!

1082
01:41:12,275 --> 01:41:13,984
What's happening?

1083
01:41:21,742 --> 01:41:23,451
Buddha, come away from the door.

1084
01:41:23,619 --> 01:41:25,120
Come, Buddha, come!

1085
01:41:25,997 --> 01:41:28,582
More books! More books!

1086
01:42:22,219 --> 01:42:24,387
How long have I been out of it?

1087
01:42:25,514 --> 01:42:27,223
Couple hours.

1088
01:42:27,391 --> 01:42:28,892
How do you feel?

1089
01:42:29,060 --> 01:42:31,895
Okay. What happened?

1090
01:42:32,063 --> 01:42:36,775
Well, we had to get inside in kind of
a hurry, so I sort of pushed you in.

1091
01:42:37,902 --> 01:42:40,028
I should be used to you
pushing me around.

1092
01:42:41,530 --> 01:42:43,490
Good to have you back.

1093
01:42:48,788 --> 01:42:50,497
What are you doing?

1094
01:42:51,666 --> 01:42:55,835
The eye of the storm has passed,
and we're 40 miles from Manhattan.

1095
01:42:56,003 --> 01:42:57,712
Shouldn't we wait one more day?

1096
01:43:00,508 --> 01:43:03,093
Sam may not have one more day.

1097
01:44:15,166 --> 01:44:17,667
What do you think's
gonna happen to us?

1098
01:44:18,043 --> 01:44:19,878
What do you mean?

1099
01:44:21,130 --> 01:44:25,675
I mean us. Civilization. Everybody.

1100
01:44:27,845 --> 01:44:30,930
Mankind survived the last Ice Age.

1101
01:44:31,098 --> 01:44:34,350
We're certainly capable
of surviving this one.

1102
01:44:35,686 --> 01:44:39,189
All depends on whether or not
we're able to learn from our mistakes.

1103
01:44:41,776 --> 01:44:45,069
I sure as hell would like a chance
to learn from mine.

1104
01:44:46,197 --> 01:44:48,490
You did everything you could.

1105
01:44:51,535 --> 01:44:53,661
I was thinking about Sam.

1106
01:44:59,627 --> 01:45:02,545
Jack, you know the chances of Sam...

1107
01:45:14,141 --> 01:45:16,643
I made my son a promise.

1108
01:45:17,853 --> 01:45:19,729
I'm going to keep it.

1109
01:45:43,379 --> 01:45:46,339
Parker, this is Houston. Do you read?

1110
01:45:46,507 --> 01:45:48,466
Roger, mission control. Go ahead.

1111
01:45:48,634 --> 01:45:52,512
We've got scattered reports that
the storm is dissipating over North America.

1112
01:45:52,930 --> 01:45:54,889
Can you confirm?

1113
01:45:56,267 --> 01:45:59,769
Affirmative. It's finally clearing.

1114
01:46:00,437 --> 01:46:01,980
We're over Europe right now.

1115
01:46:02,147 --> 01:46:05,316
I can see landmass
for the first time in days.

1116
01:47:55,552 --> 01:47:58,012
How much further is it to the library?

1117
01:47:58,180 --> 01:48:00,014
It should be...

1118
01:48:02,810 --> 01:48:04,644
...right here.

1119
01:48:08,899 --> 01:48:10,942
I'm sorry, Jack.

1120
01:48:48,689 --> 01:48:50,356
Sam?

1121
01:49:59,718 --> 01:50:01,302
Who is that?

1122
01:50:07,184 --> 01:50:09,102
My father.

1123
01:50:13,941 --> 01:50:15,358
You made it.

1124
01:50:19,738 --> 01:50:21,656
Of course I did.

1125
01:51:12,583 --> 01:51:14,542
Mr. President.

1126
01:51:16,587 --> 01:51:19,922
I've just received a shortwave
radio transmission from Jack Hall.

1127
01:51:20,090 --> 01:51:22,133
He made it to New York.

1128
01:51:22,885 --> 01:51:24,719
He says there are survivors.

1129
01:51:26,471 --> 01:51:28,222
Thank you, Tom.

1130
01:51:29,266 --> 01:51:30,808
That's...

1131
01:51:31,810 --> 01:51:33,644
That's good news.

1132
01:51:43,071 --> 01:51:48,117
These past few weeks have left us all
with a profound sense of humility...

1133
01:51:48,285 --> 01:51:51,579
...in the face of nature's
destructive power.

1134
01:51:51,747 --> 01:51:55,458
For years, we operated under the belief
that we could continue...

1135
01:51:55,626 --> 01:52:01,255
...consuming our planet's natural resources
without consequence.

1136
01:52:02,174 --> 01:52:03,925
We were wrong.

1137
01:52:05,344 --> 01:52:07,178
I was wrong.

1138
01:52:08,138 --> 01:52:12,266
The fact that my first address to you
comes from a consulate on foreign soil...

1139
01:52:12,684 --> 01:52:16,062
...is a testament to our changed reality.

1140
01:52:16,355 --> 01:52:18,147
Not only Americans...

1141
01:52:18,440 --> 01:52:21,817
...but people all around the globe
are now guests in the nations...

1142
01:52:21,985 --> 01:52:24,820
...we once called The Third World.

1143
01:52:24,988 --> 01:52:29,325
In our time of need,
they have taken us in and sheltered us.

1144
01:52:29,952 --> 01:52:33,537
And I am deeply grateful
for their hospitality.

1145
01:52:55,060 --> 01:52:59,230
We mourn the loss of a spirited leader
whose courageous order to evacuate...

1146
01:52:59,398 --> 01:53:04,151
Peter? Hey, you feeling okay today?

1147
01:53:04,319 --> 01:53:07,196
For days, we've despaired
about the fate of the people...

1148
01:53:07,364 --> 01:53:09,699
...who are trapped in the North.

1149
01:53:09,866 --> 01:53:13,369
Today, there is cause for hope.

1150
01:53:14,413 --> 01:53:17,999
Only a few hours ago, I received word
that a small group of people...

1151
01:53:18,166 --> 01:53:21,460
...survived in New York City
against all odds...

1152
01:53:21,628 --> 01:53:24,463
...and in the face
of tremendous adversity.

1153
01:53:24,631 --> 01:53:27,341
I've ordered an immediate
search-and-rescue mission...

1154
01:53:27,509 --> 01:53:31,387
...to bring them home
and to look for more survivors.

1155
01:54:02,210 --> 01:54:03,711
Jack!

1156
01:54:04,171 --> 01:54:07,673
It's good to see you.
Come on, let's go get on board.

1157
01:55:02,979 --> 01:55:04,980
Look at that.

1158
01:55:05,315 --> 01:55:06,774
What?

1159
01:55:07,901 --> 01:55:10,903
Have you ever seen the air so clear?

